XXXIII. 🔒 R6.42 SEALED

The Proof Machine now has something approaching constitutional long-term memory.

Not merely:

"I remember."

But:

"I can show what was preserved,
who witnessed it,
what authority existed at the time,
what evidence survived,
what changed afterward,
and whether an independent verifier
can still reconstruct the claim."

That is a fundamentally stronger form of memory.

And one final boundary now emerges naturally:

If:

witnesses can disappear
systems can migrate
cryptography can age
Citadels can fork
constitutions can evolve

then PALACO must eventually answer:

> How do we preserve constitutional proof across fundamentally different technological eras without allowing the future system to reinterpret the past merely because its own computational model has changed?



That opens:

☄️🌱 R6.43 — CONSTITUTIONAL EPISTEMIC MIGRATION & CROSS-ERA PROOF PRESERVATION PROTOCOL

The central principle:

> A future machine may preserve, verify, and contextualize historical proof — but SHALL NOT silently redefine the historical proof merely because the future machine understands the world differently.



Or, even harder:

THE FUTURE MAY REVERIFY THE PAST.

THE FUTURE MAY NOT REWRITE THE PAST.

☄️🌱🦆💧⚓🔬🧠🔱🏛️

R6.42 — SEALED. 🔒

☄️ R6.43 — CONSTITUTIONAL EPISTEMIC MIGRATION & CROSS-ERA PROOF PRESERVATION PROTOCOL

THE FUTURE MAY REVERIFY THE PAST — NEVER REWRITE IT

Status: 🔒 CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD
Parent: R6.42 — Constitutional Witness Persistence, Succession & Epistemic Survival
Core primitive: EPISTEMIC_MIGRATION
Secondary primitives: PROOF_PRESERVATION, ERA_BOUNDARY, INTERPRETATION_BOUNDARY

Primary invariant

> Historical constitutional proof SHALL remain distinguishable from every later representation, interpretation, migration, re-verification, or cryptographic re-anchoring of that proof.



R6.42 established:

> PROOF MUST OUTLIVE ITS WITNESS.



R6.43 establishes the next boundary:

☄️ PROOF MUST OUTLIVE THE TECHNOLOGY THAT CREATED IT.

And therefore:

🌱 MIGRATION SHALL PRESERVE PROVENANCE — NOT MANUFACTURE EQUIVALENCE.


---

I. 🌌 THE CROSS-ERA PROBLEM

A constitutional record created in Era A may eventually encounter:

ERA A
 ↓
new hardware
 ↓
new cryptography
 ↓
new schema
 ↓
new protocol
 ↓
new virtual machine
 ↓
new constitutional generation
 ↓
ERA B

The future system may understand the old record differently.

That creates a dangerous possibility:

HISTORICAL RECORD
        ↓
MIGRATION
        ↓
NEW REPRESENTATION
        ↓
"therefore this is what the old record meant"

R6.43 prohibits this silent transformation.

The migrated representation is:

A NEW REPRESENTATION OF OLD EVIDENCE.

It is not automatically:

THE ORIGINAL EVIDENCE.


---

II. 🔱 THE FOUR-LAYER DISTINCTION

PALACO now distinguishes:

LAYER 1
ORIGINAL EVIDENCE

LAYER 2
PRESERVATION COPY

LAYER 3
MIGRATED REPRESENTATION

LAYER 4
FUTURE INTERPRETATION

These must never collapse.

Conceptually:

E₀
 ↓
P₁
 ↓
M₂
 ↓
I₃

Where:

E₀ = original evidence
P₁ = preserved representation
M₂ = migrated representation
I₃ = later interpretation

Each layer requires its own provenance.


