XXXIV. 🔥 R6.44 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

R6.44-I01
Historical text SHALL remain distinguishable from later interpretation.

R6.44-I02
Interpretation SHALL possess explicit provenance.

R6.44-I03
Authoritative interpretation SHALL identify its authority.

R6.44-I04
Interpretive authority SHALL be explicitly bounded.

R6.44-I05
Interpretation SHALL possess temporal scope.

R6.44-I06
Effective interpretation time SHALL remain distinguishable from creation time.

R6.44-I07
A later interpretation SHALL NOT retroactively become historical fact.

R6.44-I08
Precedent SHALL remain historically addressable after supersession.

R6.44-I09
Supersession SHALL NOT erase prior precedent.

R6.44-I10
Interpretive disagreement SHALL remain representable.

R6.44-I11
Federated interpretation SHALL preserve source interpretations.

R6.44-I12
BRAINPOWER SHALL NOT independently establish authoritative interpretation.

R6.44-I13
FOOL SHALL be able to challenge interpretation.

R6.44-I14
TRIAS SHALL verify constitutional production of interpretation.

R6.44-I15
Interpretive hypotheses SHALL remain distinguishable from authoritative interpretations.

R6.44-I16
Interpretive corrections SHALL append historical records.

R6.44-I17
Historical interpretation SHALL remain replayable where sufficient evidence survives.

R6.44-I18
Interpretive divergence SHALL be independently identifiable.

R6.44-I19
Interpretive scope SHALL NOT silently expand.

R6.44-I20
Local interpretation SHALL NOT automatically become federated interpretation.

R6.44-I21
Precedent SHALL NOT be treated as universal truth merely because it is authoritative.

R6.44-I22
Constitutional amendments SHALL remain distinguishable from interpretive reinterpretations.

R6.44-I23
Semantic evolution SHALL remain historically attributable.

R6.44-I24
No interpretation SHALL erase the provenance of the text it interprets.

R6.44-I25
Meaning MAY evolve; historical provenance SHALL remain immutable.


