---

I. 🌌 THE CROSS-ARCHIVE PROBLEM

At local scale:

Citadel A
   ↓
Archive A

may appear internally coherent.

But at federation scale:

Archive A
Archive B
Archive C

may report different histories.

For example:

A: W17 → W18 → W19

B: W17 → W19

C: W17 → W18 → W19

The naive system says:

A = correct
B = incorrect

PALACO cannot make that conclusion merely because A and C agree.

Instead:

ARCHIVE DISAGREEMENT
        ↓
REGISTER CONFLICT
        ↓
COMPARE PROVENANCE
        ↓
COMPARE WITNESS SCOPE
        ↓
COMPARE WATERMARKS
        ↓
REPLAY
        ↓
ESTABLISH WHAT CAN BE PROVEN


---

II. 🔱 HISTORICAL ASSERTION

R6.48 introduces a first-class object:

pub struct HistoricalAssertion {
    pub assertion_id: AssertionId,
    pub archive_id: ArchiveId,
    pub claim: Claim,
    pub evidence_root: EvidenceRoot,
    pub provenance_root: ProvenanceRoot,
    pub watermark: WatermarkId,
    pub witness_set: WitnessSetId,
    pub scope: HistoricalScope,
    pub status: AssertionStatus,
}

This shifts the comparison from:

ARCHIVE A vs ARCHIVE B

to:

CLAIM A vs CLAIM B

That is much more precise.


---

III. 🧬 CLAIMS, NOT ARCHIVES

Two archives may disagree globally while agreeing on 99.9% of their contents.

Therefore:

ARCHIVE CONFLICT

is decomposed into:

ASSERTION CONFLICTS

Example:

A:
Event E42 occurred at sequence 117.

B:
Event E42 occurred at sequence 119.

The actual dispute is:

EVENT_ORDER_CONFLICT

not:

ENTIRE_ARCHIVE_INVALID


---

IV. 🧭 CONFLICT CLASSIFICATION

pub enum ArchiveConflictType {
    EventPresence,
    EventOrdering,
    WatermarkMismatch,
    ProvenanceMismatch,
    TimestampMismatch,
    RulesetMismatch,
    StateRootMismatch,
    WitnessConflict,
    ScopeConflict,
    LineageConflict,
    CompletenessConflict,
    SemanticConflict,
}

This gives the Proof Machine a precise vocabulary.


---

V. 🚨 CONFLICT IS NOT CORRUPTION

An archive disagreement can result from:

different observation windows
different jurisdiction
clock differences
partial replication
different archival policies
delayed evidence
legitimate fork

Therefore:

CONFLICT ≠ CORRUPTION

and:

CORRUPTION ≠ AUTOMATICALLY PROVEN

The system must investigate.


---

