XXXI. ☄️ THE DEEP PRINCIPLE

R6.48 establishes:

DISAGREEMENT IS DATA.

If two independent archives disagree, that disagreement itself tells us something:

different observation
different scope
different lineage
different state
different interpretation
or potentially
different truth claim

The first responsibility of the Proof Machine is therefore not to eliminate disagreement.

It is to characterize it accurately.


---

XXXII. 🔱 R6.48 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

R6.48-I01
Conflicting archives SHALL remain independently identifiable.

R6.48-I02
Archive disagreement SHALL NOT automatically invalidate either archive.

R6.48-I03
Historical assertions SHALL be evaluated independently of archive reputation.

R6.48-I04
Provenance SHALL be considered in cross-archive reconciliation.

R6.48-I05
Witness scope SHALL be explicitly preserved.

R6.48-I06
Witness independence SHALL be evaluated where relevant.

R6.48-I07
Watermark equivalence SHALL require cryptographically verifiable equivalence.

R6.48-I08
Similarity SHALL NOT be treated as equivalence.

R6.48-I09
Historical forks SHALL remain explicitly representable.

R6.48-I10
Forks SHALL NOT be silently collapsed into a single history.

R6.48-I11
Majority preference SHALL NOT by itself establish historical truth.

R6.48-I12
BRAINPOWER SHALL generate reconciliation candidates but SHALL NOT fabricate historical facts.

R6.48-I13
FOOL SHALL be able to challenge cross-archive reconciliation.

R6.48-I14
TRIAS SHALL verify reconciliation methodology and evidence.

R6.48-I15
Replay SHALL be used where required to test state-history consistency.

R6.48-I16
The first cross-archive divergence SHALL be independently identifiable where replay permits.

R6.48-I17
Federated watermarks SHALL preserve their source watermarks.

R6.48-I18
Reconciliation SHALL create a new historical proof object rather than overwrite source histories.

R6.48-I19
Reconciliation results SHALL be immutable after sealing.

R6.48-I20
New evidence SHALL produce a new reconciliation transition.

R6.48-I21
Historical uncertainty SHALL remain explicitly representable.

R6.48-I22
Probability SHALL NOT automatically constitute provenance.

R6.48-I23
Uncomparable archives SHALL NOT be forced into false equivalence.

R6.48-I24
Scope-separated histories SHALL remain scope-separated.

R6.48-I25
Where proof ends, PALACO SHALL preserve uncertainty rather than fabricate continuity.


