XXXVII. 🔒 R6.49 SEALED

PALACO can now legitimately distinguish three statements:

1. PROVEN

> The available evidence establishes the proposition under the applicable verification rules.



2. CLOSED

> The constitutional process has reached its defined stopping threshold for governance.



3. REOPENABLE

> Materially new, independently verifiable evidence can reopen the question.



These can coexist.

PROVEN
+
CLOSED
+
REOPENABLE

is therefore not contradictory.

It is the intended state of a mature epistemic system.


---

☄️🌱 THE NEXT FRONTIER — R6.50

And now the architecture reaches a genuinely consequential boundary.

PALACO can:

prove events;

anchor them in time;

establish lineage;

preserve witnesses;

preserve dissent;

preserve archives;

reconcile conflicting histories;

represent uncertainty;

close questions;

reopen them when evidence warrants.


But one question remains:

> What happens when the constitutional system itself changes?



Not merely a software version.

Not merely a schema migration.

But a genuine constitutional transition:

CONSTITUTION A
      ↓
      ?
CONSTITUTION B

What survives?

Which authority survives?

Which precedents survive?

Which Watermarks remain valid?

Which rules may interpret the old history?

And most importantly:

HOW DOES A NEW CONSTITUTION INHERIT A PAST IT DID NOT CREATE?

That is the natural threshold of:

☄️ R6.50 — CONSTITUTIONAL SUCCESSION, ANCESTRAL INHERITANCE & CROSS-GENERATION AUTHORITY TRANSFER PROTOCOL

The governing principle:

> A successor constitution MAY establish new authority, but SHALL inherit historical facts only through proven lineage, explicit succession rules, and preserved constitutional memory.



Or, in its shortest form:

🔱 A NEW CONSTITUTION MAY INHERIT THE PAST. IT MAY NOT REAUTHOR THE PAST.

☄️🌱🦆💧⚓🔬🧠🔱🏛️💀❤

R6.49 — SEALED. 🔒

☄️ R6.50 — CONSTITUTIONAL SUCCESSION, ANCESTRAL INHERITANCE & CROSS-GENERATION AUTHORITY TRANSFER PROTOCOL

THE NEW CONSTITUTION INHERITS HISTORY — IT DOES NOT REWRITE IT

Status: 🔒 CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD
Parent: R6.49 — Constitutional Epistemic Closure, Reopening Thresholds & Proof Finality
Core primitive: CONSTITUTIONAL_SUCCESSION
Primary invariant:

> A successor constitution SHALL NOT acquire historical authority merely by succeeding a predecessor. Every inherited constitutional claim SHALL be traceable to an explicit succession rule, preserved provenance, and verifiable historical lineage.



R6.19 established:

> NO LINK → NO CLAIM OF CONTINUITY.



R6.49 established:

> CLOSURE IS NOT OMNISCIENCE.



R6.50 now establishes:

🔱 SUCCESSION IS NOT REWRITE.


---

I. 🌌 THE GENERATIONAL PROBLEM

Eventually every constitutional system changes.

CONSTITUTION A
      ↓
   SUCCESSION
      ↓
CONSTITUTION B

The dangerous assumption is:

B exists
↓
therefore
B owns A's history

That is forbidden.

A successor may inherit:

historical records
provenance
watermarks
precedents
obligations
archives

but inheritance must itself be proven.

Therefore:

HISTORY PRECEDES SUCCESSION.


---

II. 🧬 CONSTITUTIONAL GENERATION

R6.50 introduces the concept of a constitutional generation:

pub struct ConstitutionalGeneration {
    pub generation_id: GenerationId,
    pub constitution_id: ConstitutionId,
    pub predecessor: Option<GenerationId>,
    pub genesis_watermark: WatermarkId,
    pub succession_proof: Option<SuccessionProofId>,
    pub ruleset_hash: RuleSetHash,
    pub effective_at: Timestamp,
    pub status: GenerationStatus,
}

A generation is not simply a software version.

It represents a constitutionally bounded era of authority.


---

III. 💧 SUCCESSION WATERMARK

The transition receives its own anchor:

OLD CONSTITUTION
       ↓
FINAL WATERMARK
       ↓
SUCCESSION EVENT
       ↓
SUCCESSION PROOF
       ↓
NEW CONSTITUTION
       ↓
GENESIS WATERMARK

Thus:

W_old_final
      ↓
S
      ↓
W_new_genesis

The new generation does not magically appear.

Its ancestry is explicit.


---

IV. 🔐 SUCCESSION PROOF

Conceptually:

pub struct SuccessionProof {
    pub proof_id: SuccessionProofId,
    pub predecessor_constitution: ConstitutionId,
    pub successor_constitution: ConstitutionId,
    pub predecessor_watermark: WatermarkId,
    pub succession_event: EventId,
    pub authority_basis: AuthorityReference,
    pub evidence_root: EvidenceRoot,
    pub provenance_root: ProvenanceRoot,
    pub challenge_root: ChallengeRoot,
    pub verification_root: VerificationRoot,
    pub successor_watermark: WatermarkId,
}

This answers:

> Why is B constitutionally recognized as successor to A?




---

V. 🚫 SUCCESSION CANNOT CREATE ANCESTRY

Forbidden:

CONSTITUTION B
↓
declares
↓
"we have always existed"

A successor cannot manufacture:

W₀
W₁
W₂

that belong to an earlier generation.

It may reference them.

It may inherit them.

It may reinterpret their legal significance where authorized.

But:

IT MAY NOT ALTER THEIR HISTORICAL EXISTENCE.


---

VI. 🕰️ THREE DIFFERENT THINGS

R6.50 sharply separates:

Historical fact

What happened?

Constitutional validity at the time

Was it valid under Constitution A?

Successor interpretation

What significance does Constitution B assign to that historical fact?

These must never be conflated.

Example:

E42 happened under A.

B cannot retroactively make:

E42 never happened.

It may instead declare:

E42 remains historically valid,
but no longer carries operative authority under B.

That is legitimate succession.


---

