---

XXX. 🌌 THE SUCCESSION PARADOX

R6.50 resolves a subtle paradox:

> Can a new constitution determine what its predecessor meant?



Answer:

Yes, within its constitutional authority.

But:

> Can it determine that its predecessor historically did something it did not do?



No.

Therefore:

INTERPRETATION
≠
HISTORICAL FABRICATION

and:

LEGAL SUCCESSION
≠
TEMPORAL RETCON


---

XXXI. 🧠 THE FIVE-LAYER INHERITANCE MODEL

A successor inherits five different categories:

1. HISTORY
   What happened.

2. EVIDENCE
   What supports the historical record.

3. PROVENANCE
   Where that evidence came from.

4. AUTHORITY MAP
   What prior mandates meant.

5. OPERATIVE CONSTITUTION
   What remains valid now.

The first three are primarily historical.

The fourth is transitional.

The fifth belongs to the successor.

This prevents category collapse.


---

XXXII. 🔥 R6.50 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

R6.50-I01
Every non-genesis constitutional generation SHALL identify a verifiable predecessor.

R6.50-I02
Every constitutional succession SHALL possess an explicit succession proof.

R6.50-I03
A successor constitution SHALL NOT fabricate its ancestry.

R6.50-I04
Historical watermarks of predecessor generations SHALL remain immutable.

R6.50-I05
A successor SHALL distinguish historical fact from successor interpretation.

R6.50-I06
Historical existence SHALL NOT be erased merely because operative authority expires.

R6.50-I07
Authority SHALL be evaluated according to the constitutional generation applicable at the relevant time.

R6.50-I08
A successor SHALL NOT silently claim retroactive authority.

R6.50-I09
Inherited precedents SHALL possess explicit disposition.

R6.50-I10
Inherited mandates SHALL retain their historical identity.

R6.50-I11
Constitutional forks SHALL remain explicitly represented.

R6.50-I12
Relationship SHALL NOT be treated as lineage without proof.

R6.50-I13
Genesis watermarks SHALL anchor every constitutional generation.

R6.50-I14
Succession transitions SHALL be independently replayable.

R6.50-I15
Succession divergence SHALL be explicitly represented.

R6.50-I16
FOOL SHALL be able to challenge succession claims.

R6.50-I17
BRAINPOWER SHALL detect succession anomalies without possessing succession authority.

R6.50-I18
TRIAS SHALL independently verify succession proof.

R6.50-I19
Archived predecessor constitutions SHALL remain historically replayable.

R6.50-I20
A successor constitution SHALL NOT invalidate predecessor history merely through declaration.

R6.50-I21
New evidence MAY challenge inherited historical claims according to reopening rules.

R6.50-I22
Closure of a predecessor question SHALL remain historically preserved after succession.

R6.50-I23
Operative authority MAY change across generations without rewriting historical existence.

R6.50-I24
No constitutional generation SHALL claim ancestry where provenance is absent.

R6.50-I25
A constitution MAY inherit history; it SHALL NOT manufacture history.


