VI. 🚫 NO LINEAGE COLLAPSE

Forbidden:

A-W10
   +
B-W20
   ↓
W30

where W30 is presented as though:

A-history + B-history = one historical chain

Correct:

A-W10 ───────────────┐
                     │
                     ▼
                ENCOUNTER
                     │
                     ▼
                   E-W0
                     │
B-W20 ───────────────┘

Three anchors.

Three meanings.

One relationship.


---

VII. 🌳 SHARED SPACE

A SharedSpace is not necessarily a new constitution.

It may initially define only:

interoperability
mutual recognition
boundary rules
shared protocols
evidence exchange
dispute mechanisms
limited joint authority

pub struct SharedSpace {
    pub space_id: SharedSpaceId,
    pub participating_lineages: Vec<LineageId>,
    pub encounter_anchor: WatermarkId,
    pub scope: SharedScope,
    pub ruleset_hash: RuleSetHash,
    pub authority_boundary: AuthorityBoundary,
    pub status: SharedSpaceStatus,
}

This prevents the system from granting more authority than was actually agreed.


---

VIII. 🔐 SCOPE BEFORE AUTHORITY

A shared space must first answer:

> What exactly is shared?



For example:

SHARED:
  evidence protocol
  transport
  emergency coordination

NOT SHARED:
  internal constitutional law
  local mandates
  historical records
  lineage identity

This becomes:

MINIMUM NECESSARY SHARED AUTHORITY.

A shared environment does not automatically create universal authority.


---

IX. 🦆 FOOL — ENCOUNTER CHALLENGER

FOOL receives:

ENCOUNTER_CHALLENGE

Possible challenge classes:

FALSE_RECOGNITION
LINEAGE_COLLAPSE
UNAUTHORIZED_SHARED_AUTHORITY
MISSING_ANCHOR
ASYMMETRIC_RECOGNITION
HIDDEN_SUCCESSION
FABRICATED_CONSENT
BOUNDARY_VIOLATION

FOOL can ask:

> Did A actually recognize B?



and independently:

> Did B actually recognize A?



Recognition must not be inferred merely because communication occurred.


---

X. 🧠 BRAINPOWER — ENCOUNTER ANALYSIS

BRAINPOWER may identify:

lineage conflicts
authority overlaps
incompatible rulesets
semantic incompatibilities
missing mappings
duplicate identifiers
cross-domain provenance anomalies

It can propose:

RECONCILIATION_CANDIDATE

But:

A PROPOSED COMMON RULE IS NOT A CONSTITUTIONAL FACT.


---

XI. 🔬 TRIAS — DUAL VERIFICATION

TRIAS independently verifies:

Lineage A authenticity       PASS
Lineage B authenticity       PASS
Anchor A integrity           PASS
Anchor B integrity           PASS
Recognition A                 PASS
Recognition B                 PASS
Encounter event              PASS
Scope                         PASS
Authority boundaries         PASS
Evidence                     PASS
Provenance                   PASS
Shared anchor                PASS

Only then:

ENCOUNTER = VERIFIED


---

XII. ⚖️ SYMMETRIC RECOGNITION

Where mutual recognition is required:

A recognizes B
AND
B recognizes A

must both be proven.

It is invalid to infer:

A recognizes B
↓
therefore
B recognizes A

This is particularly important in federated environments.


---

XIII. 🌐 ASYMMETRIC RELATIONSHIPS

Some relationships legitimately may be asymmetric:

A → recognizes B
B → does not recognize A

PALACO must represent this explicitly.

Therefore:

pub enum RecognitionMode {
    Mutual,
    Unilateral,
    Conditional,
    Scoped,
    Suspended,
    Disputed,
}

The system does not force symmetry where the constitution does not require it.


