That requires formal constitutional jurisdiction, authority domains, boundary proofs, and cross-domain delegation controls.

The next principle therefore emerges:

🔱 AUTHORITY DOES NOT CROSS A CONSTITUTIONAL BORDER WITHOUT PROOF.

R6.53 — CONSTITUTIONAL JURISDICTION, BOUNDARY PROOF & CROSS-DOMAIN AUTHORITY CONTROL PROTOCOL

And that takes the architecture from constitutional diplomacy into constitutional sovereignty and jurisdiction.

☄️🔱 R6.53 — CONSTITUTIONAL JURISDICTION, BOUNDARY PROOF & CROSS-DOMAIN AUTHORITY CONTROL PROTOCOL

AUTHORITY DOES NOT CROSS A CONSTITUTIONAL BORDER WITHOUT PROOF

Status: 🔒 CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD
Parent: R6.52 — Constitutional Encounter, Dual-Lineage Recognition & Shared-Space Formation
Core primitive: JURISDICTION_BOUNDARY
Secondary primitives: AUTHORITY_SCOPE, BOUNDARY_PROOF, CROSS_DOMAIN_DELEGATION

Primary invariant

> No constitutional authority SHALL operate outside its proven jurisdictional scope unless an explicit, valid, provenance-bound delegation or recognition authorizes that operation.



R6.50 established:

> INHERITANCE IS NOT ERASURE.



R6.51 established:

> NO ANCESTOR → GENESIS PROOF.



R6.52 established:

> MEETING ≠ MERGING.



R6.53 now establishes:

🔱 AUTHORITY ≠ REACH.


---

I. 🌌 THE JURISDICTION PROBLEM

Two constitutional domains may coexist:

┌───────────────────────┐
│      LINEAGE A        │
│                       │
│      AUTHORITY A      │
└───────────────────────┘

┌───────────────────────┐
│      LINEAGE B        │
│                       │
│      AUTHORITY B      │
└───────────────────────┘

They may share infrastructure.

They may communicate.

They may reconcile evidence.

They may form a federation.

None of this automatically means:

A → authority over B

or:

B → authority over A

Therefore PALACO must make jurisdiction explicit.


---

II. 🧭 JURISDICTION OBJECT

pub struct Jurisdiction {
    pub jurisdiction_id: JurisdictionId,
    pub lineage_id: LineageId,
    pub authority_scope: AuthorityScope,
    pub boundary: BoundaryDefinition,
    pub ruleset_hash: RuleSetHash,
    pub provenance_root: ProvenanceRoot,
    pub evidence_root: EvidenceRoot,
    pub effective_watermark: WatermarkId,
    pub expiry: Option<Timestamp>,
    pub status: JurisdictionStatus,
}

A jurisdiction is therefore not merely a geographic or logical region.

It is:

WHO
+
MAY DO WHAT
+
WHERE
+
UNDER WHICH RULES
+
FOR HOW LONG


---

III. 🔐 AUTHORITY SCOPE

Authority must be typed.

pub struct AuthorityScope {
    pub action_classes: ActionClassSet,
    pub resource_classes: ResourceClassSet,
    pub domains: DomainSet,
    pub constraints: ConstraintSet,
    pub temporal_scope: TemporalScope,
}

Thus an authority might be:

ALLOW:
    evidence verification

WITHIN:
    shared-space S1

DURING:
    emergency window E7

UNDER:
    ruleset R44

It does not automatically become:

ALLOW:
    arbitrary actions


---

IV. 🚫 NO IMPLIED SOVEREIGNTY

A system must never reason:

A participates in federation F
↓
therefore
A controls F

Nor:

A hosts infrastructure for B
↓
therefore
A owns B's constitutional authority

Nor:

A can observe B
↓
therefore
A may command B

These are distinct capabilities.

OBSERVATION ≠ AUTHORITY.


---

V. 🌐 SHARED SPACE BOUNDARY

R6.52 introduced shared space.

R6.53 now gives it a perimeter:

SHARED SPACE
        ┌───────────────────┐
        │                   │
   A ───┤  permitted scope ├── B
        │                   │
        └───────────────────┘

Each boundary must answer:

What enters?
What exits?
Who may act?
Who may verify?
Who may delegate?
Who may revoke?


---

VI. 💧 BOUNDARY WATERMARK

Every material jurisdictional transition receives a Watermark reference:

A-Wn
  ↓
AUTHORITY_GRANT
  ↓
BOUNDARY_PROOF
  ↓
B-Wm

Or:

SHARED-W5
    ↓
DELEGATION D7
    ↓
BOUNDARY-W6

Thus authority movement becomes historically reconstructible.


---

VII. 🔱 CROSS-DOMAIN DELEGATION

Authority may legitimately cross a boundary.

But only through explicit delegation:

DOMAIN A
   │
   ▼
DELEGATION
   │
   ├── scope
   ├── purpose
   ├── duration
   ├── constraints
   └── evidence
   │
   ▼
DOMAIN B

Conceptually:

pub struct Delegation {
    pub delegation_id: DelegationId,
    pub issuer: AuthorityId,
    pub recipient: AuthorityId,
    pub source_domain: DomainId,
    pub target_domain: DomainId,
    pub scope: AuthorityScope,
    pub constraints: ConstraintSet,
    pub valid_from: Timestamp,
    pub valid_until: Option<Timestamp>,
    pub evidence_root: EvidenceRoot,
    pub provenance_root: ProvenanceRoot,
    pub signature: Signature,
}


---

VIII. 🧬 DELEGATION IS NOT TRANSFER OF IDENTITY

If:

A delegates X to B

this does not mean:

B becomes A.

Nor:

B inherits A's lineage.

Nor:

B inherits A's constitutional identity.

It means only:

B receives authority X
within the proven delegation scope.

Therefore:

DELEGATED AUTHORITY ≠ CONSTITUTIONAL SUCCESSION.


---

IX. ⏳ TEMPORAL DELEGATION

Authority may be temporary:

D7
│
├── START = T100
└── END   = T200

After T200:

D7 = EXPIRED

The historical fact that D7 existed remains.

Therefore:

EXPIRED AUTHORITY
≠
ERASED AUTHORITY

Exactly the same memory principle applies.


---

X. 🚨 EXPIRED AUTHORITY

An expired delegation must not continue to authorize action.

If:

T > expiry

then:

AUTHORITY_CHECK = FAIL

and:

ACTION = BLOCK

unless a new delegation is established.

No silent extension.


---

XI. 🦆 FOOL — JURISDICTION CHALLENGER

FOOL receives:

JURISDICTION_CHALLENGE

Possible challenge classes:

OUT_OF_SCOPE_ACTION
UNAUTHORIZED_DELEGATION
BOUNDARY_BYPASS
EXPIRED_AUTHORITY
WRONG_DOMAIN
FORGED_DELEGATION
HIDDEN_ESCALATION
UNPROVEN_RECOGNITION
SCOPE_AMBIGUITY
RETROACTIVE_AUTHORITY

FOOL asks the crucial question:

> Where did this authority come from?



And then:

> Where does it end?




---

XII. 🧠 BRAINPOWER

BRAINPOWER may detect:

authority overlap
scope collision
boundary anomalies
delegation chains
expired grants
recursive delegation
privilege amplification
cross-domain inconsistencies

It may suggest:

POSSIBLE_BOUNDARY_VIOLATION

But:

DETECTION ≠ ADJUDICATION.


---

XIII. 🔬 TRIAS

TRIAS verifies:

Issuer identity             PASS
Recipient identity          PASS
Source jurisdiction         PASS
Target jurisdiction         PASS
Scope                       PASS
Temporal validity           PASS
Evidence                    PASS
Provenance                  PASS
Delegation signature        PASS
Boundary compatibility      PASS
Revocation status           PASS
Ruleset binding             PASS

Only then:

DELEGATION = VALID


---

XIV. 💀 AUTHORITY ESCALATION

A dangerous chain:

A
↓
delegates X to B
↓
B
↓
delegates Y to C

must not silently produce:

C = X + Y

Delegation must obey:

NO DELEGATE MAY GRANT MORE AUTHORITY THAN IT POSSESSES.

Conceptually:

Scope(C) ⊆ Scope(B)

unless B possesses a separately proven constitutional mechanism allowing subdelegation.


---

XV. 🔒 NON-ESCALATION

PALACO therefore adds:

R6.53-NO-ESCALATION

A delegation cannot increase:

authority breadth
authority depth
authority duration
authority domain

without independent authorization.

This extends the previously established Zero Privilege Escalation invariant into federated jurisdiction.


---

XVI. 🌐 MULTI-HOP DELEGATION

Consider:

A
 ↓ D1
B
 ↓ D2
C
 ↓ D3
D

The Proof Machine must reconstruct the entire chain:

A → B → C → D

and determine:

effective_scope(D)

If one link fails:

D3 ✓
D2 ✕
D1 ✓

then the downstream authority is:

UNPROVEN

not:

PROBABLY_VALID


---

XVII. 💧 WATERMARKED AUTHORITY

Every significant authority change becomes:

AUTHORITY
    ↓
DECISION
    ↓
EVIDENCE
    ↓
PROVENANCE
    ↓
EVENT
    ↓
QUAY
    ↓
WATERMARK

Therefore PALACO can answer:

> When did this authority become valid?



> Who granted it?



> Under which rules?



> For which domain?



> When did it expire?



> Was it ever challenged?



> Was it subsequently revoked?




---

XVIII. ⚓ QUAY JURISDICTION MEMORY

The Quay stores:

jurisdiction declarations
delegations
recognitions
revocations
boundary changes
scope changes
disputes
adjudications

The history becomes:

J0
 ↓
D1
 ↓
W1
 ↓
D2
 ↓
W2
 ↓
REVOCATION
 ↓
W3

Nothing is silently overwritten.


---

XIX. 🔥 REVOCATION

Revocation is itself an event:

VALID DELEGATION
      ↓
REVOCATION EVENT
      ↓
NEW WATERMARK

The original delegation remains historical.

The operative authority changes.

Thus:

REVOCATION CHANGES THE PRESENT; IT DOES NOT DELETE THE PAST.


---

XX. ⚖️ JURISDICTIONAL CONFLICT

Suppose:

A says:
ACTION X = ALLOWED

while:

B says:
ACTION X = FORBIDDEN

and both domains have legitimate authority.

PALACO cannot simply select the most powerful domain.

It must evaluate:

scope
precedence
recognition
delegation
conflict rules
temporal validity
shared-space agreement

If unresolved:

JURISDICTIONAL_CONFLICT

The action may be blocked pending adjudication.


---

XXI. 🛑 DEFAULT DENIAL

Where constitutional authority cannot be proven:

AUTHORITY = UNKNOWN

must not become:

ALLOW

Therefore:

UNPROVEN JURISDICTION → BLOCK.

This is the natural extension of the constitutional access gate.


---

XXII. 🏛️ LA PAZ

Consider:

LA PAZ
│
├── LOCAL DOMAIN
│
└── SHARED PLANETARY SPACE

A planetary protocol may grant La Paz:

authority:
    participate in planetary evidence exchange

It does not automatically grant:

authority:
    alter La Paz's internal constitution

Unless an explicit constitutional mechanism establishes that authority.

The boundary remains:

LPZ-INTERNAL
─────────────
      │
      │ PROVEN DELEGATION
      ▼
PLANETARY-SHARED


---

XXIII. 🌐 FEDERATION

Federation becomes a collection of bounded jurisdictions:

FEDERATION
                 │
       ┌─────────┼─────────┐
       ▼         ▼         ▼
      A         B         C
   DOMAIN     DOMAIN     DOMAIN

The federation possesses only the authority explicitly granted to it.

Therefore:

FEDERATION MEMBERSHIP SHALL NOT CONFER UNBOUNDED FEDERAL AUTHORITY.

This directly reinforces the existing R6.17 principle:

> Federation membership SHALL NOT confer constitutional authority.




---

XXIV. 🧬 JURISDICTION GRAPH

R6.52 introduced typed constitutional relationships.

R6.53 adds typed authority edges:

A
│
├── RECOGNIZES → B
├── DELEGATES → C
├── SHARES → D
├── OBSERVES → E
└── VERIFIES → F

These are not equivalent.

A system that can:

OBSERVE B

does not thereby gain:

COMMAND B


---

XXV. 🔱 AUTHORITY RELATION TYPES

pub enum AuthorityRelation {
    Owns,
    Delegates,
    Subdelegates,
    Recognizes,
    Verifies,
    Observes,
    Coordinates,
    Restricts,
    Revokes,
    Suspends,
}

Every relationship requires:

scope
evidence
provenance
temporal validity

where applicable.


---

XXVI. 🚨 BOUNDARY BREACH

A boundary violation occurs when:

Action
↓
Authority
↓
Target

does not satisfy:

Target ∈ AuthorizedScope

Then:

JURISDICTION_BREACH

The Proof Machine records:

actor
authority_claim
scope
target
event
watermark
evidence

This makes the breach forensically reconstructible.


---

XXVII. 🔬 REPLAY OF JURISDICTION

The verifier reconstructs:

Replay(
    jurisdiction_state,
    delegations,
    recognitions,
    revocations,
    rulesets,
    events
)
→ AuthorityState'

Then compares:

AuthorityState'
==
WatermarkedAuthorityState

If not:

JURISDICTION_DIVERGENCE

The first divergence is identified.


---

XXVIII. 🕰️ FIRST AUTHORITY DIVERGENCE

Example:

J0 ✓
J1 ✓
J2 ✓
J3 ✕

PALACO reports:

FIRST_JURISDICTION_DIVERGENCE = J3

rather than merely:

REPLAY_FAILED

This preserves the forensic precision established in R6.19.


---

XXIX. 💧 AUTHORITY WATERMARK CHAIN

The full authority history becomes:

GENESIS
 ↓
JURISDICTION
 ↓
DELEGATION
 ↓
ACTION
 ↓
EVIDENCE
 ↓
PROVENANCE
 ↓
EVENT
 ↓
QUAY
 ↓
WATERMARK
 ↓
REVOCATION
 ↓
NEW WATERMARK

The system therefore remembers not only:

> Who had authority?



but:

> How that authority came into existence, where it applied, and how it ended.




---

XXX. 🌱 CONSTITUTIONAL BOUNDARIES AS FIRST-CLASS OBJECTS

A boundary is no longer an implicit property.

It becomes independently addressable:

pub struct ConstitutionalBoundary {
    pub boundary_id: BoundaryId,
    pub source_domain: DomainId,
    pub target_domain: DomainId,
    pub permitted_relations: RelationSet,
    pub prohibited_relations: RelationSet,
    pub proof_root: ProofRoot,
    pub effective_watermark: WatermarkId,
}

This is critical.

Because what is not explicitly bounded can eventually become ambiguous.


---

XXXI. 🚫 AMBIGUITY IS NOT AUTHORITY

PALACO now establishes:

AMBIGUOUS SCOPE
       ↓
NOT PROVEN
       ↓
NO AUTHORITY

Not:

AMBIGUOUS SCOPE
       ↓
interpret generously
       ↓
ALLOW

This prevents authority from expanding through linguistic ambiguity.


---

XXXII. 🔐 CONSTITUTIONAL FIREWALL

R6.53 effectively creates a constitutional firewall:

┌─────────────────────┐
│     DOMAIN A        │
│                     │
│    AUTHORITY A      │
└─────────┬───────────┘
          │
      PROOF GATE
          │
          ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│    SHARED SPACE     │
└─────────┬───────────┘
          │
      PROOF GATE
          │
          ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│     DOMAIN B        │
│                     │
│    AUTHORITY B      │
└─────────────────────┘

No authority crosses the firewall merely because a technical connection exists.


---

XXXIII. 🧠 BRAINPOWER + 🔬 TRIAS + 🦆 FOOL

The three functions now form a powerful jurisdictional triangle:

BRAINPOWER
                  │
             finds anomalies
                  │
                  ▼
FOOL ─────── challenges ─────── TRIAS
                                  │
                              verifies
                                  │
                                  ▼
                          CONSTITUTIONAL
                             DECISION

BRAINPOWER discovers.

FOOL challenges.

TRIAS verifies.

None may silently become the authority they are designed to examine.


---

XXXIV. 🔥 R6.53 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

R6.53-I01
Every constitutional authority SHALL possess an explicit jurisdictional scope.

R6.53-I02
Authority SHALL NOT be inferred merely from technical connectivity.

R6.53-I03
Authority SHALL NOT cross a constitutional boundary without proof.

R6.53-I04
Every cross-domain delegation SHALL identify issuer and recipient.

R6.53-I05
Every delegation SHALL specify scope.

R6.53-I06
Every delegation SHALL possess temporal validity.

R6.53-I07
Delegation SHALL NOT silently increase authority.

R6.53-I08
Subdelegation SHALL NOT exceed the delegator's proven authority.

R6.53-I09
Expired authority SHALL NOT authorize new action.

R6.53-I10
Revocation SHALL be represented as a new historical event.

R6.53-I11
Historical authority SHALL remain preserved after expiration or revocation.

R6.53-I12
Observation SHALL NOT imply command authority.

R6.53-I13
Federation membership SHALL NOT imply unrestricted jurisdiction.

R6.53-I14
Shared-space participation SHALL NOT imply sovereignty over participating lineages.

R6.53-I15
Ambiguous authority scope SHALL NOT be interpreted as authorization.

R6.53-I16
Unproven jurisdiction SHALL result in BLOCK for security-relevant action.

R6.53-I17
FOOL SHALL be able to challenge jurisdictional claims.

R6.53-I18
BRAINPOWER SHALL detect jurisdiction anomalies without granting authority.

R6.53-I19
TRIAS SHALL independently verify jurisdictional authority.

R6.53-I20
Every material authority transition SHALL be provenance-bound.

R6.53-I21
Authority transitions SHALL be replayable.

R6.53-I22
The first jurisdictional divergence SHALL be independently identifiable.

R6.53-I23
Jurisdictional boundaries SHALL remain historically auditable.

R6.53-I24
A delegation SHALL NOT constitute constitutional succession.

R6.53-I25
Authority scope SHALL remain distinct from lineage identity.

R6.53-I26
Cross-domain recognition SHALL NOT automatically create cross-domain authority.

R6.53-I27
A constitutional boundary SHALL be explicit where authority crosses domains.

R6.53-I28
No authority SHALL be created through silence, ambiguity, or technical reach alone.

R6.53-I29
All jurisdictional disputes SHALL preserve the challenged claims and evidence.

R6.53-I30
No constitutional domain SHALL silently acquire authority over another domain.


---

XXXV. 🌳 R6 CONSTITUTIONAL TOPOLOGY

The architecture now becomes:

🌱 GENESIS
                             │
                             ▼
                         💧 WATERMARK
                             │
                             ▼
                         🌳 LINEAGE
                             │
              ┌──────────────┴──────────────┐
              ▼                             ▼
        ☄️ SUCCESSION                 🌐 ENCOUNTER
              │                             │
              ▼                             ▼
        NEW GENERATION                SHARED SPACE
                                            │
                                            ▼
                                     🔱 JURISDICTION
                                            │
                                  ┌─────────┴─────────┐
                                  ▼                   ▼
                             DELEGATION          RECOGNITION
                                  │                   │
                                  └─────────┬─────────┘
                                            ▼
                                         ACTION
                                            │
                                            ▼
                                         PROOF

This is a substantial architectural transition.

PALACO is no longer merely proving state evolution.

It is proving authority topology.


---

XXXVI. ☄️ THE AUTHORITY EQUATION

Conceptually:

EffectiveAuthority =
    ConstitutionalAuthority
  ∩ Jurisdiction
  ∩ Delegation
  ∩ TemporalValidity
  ∩ Ruleset
  ∩ ResourceScope
  ∩ ActionScope

If any required component fails:

EffectiveAuthority = UNPROVEN

Therefore:

ONE BROKEN LINK CAN INVALIDATE THE AUTHORITY CLAIM.


---

XXXVII. 💧 WATERMARK → AUTHORITY

R6.19 asked:

> Where did this state come from?



R6.53 adds:

> Where did this authority come from?



The answer must now traverse:

AUTHORITY CLAIM
      ↓
CONSTITUTION
      ↓
RULESET
      ↓
JURISDICTION
      ↓
DELEGATION
      ↓
EVIDENCE
      ↓
PROVENANCE
      ↓
EVENT
      ↓
WATERMARK

That is the Authority Provenance Chain.


---

XXXVIII. 🏛️ THE LA PAZ TEST

La Paz may operate simultaneously in:

LOCAL JURISDICTION
        +
SHARED PLANETARY JURISDICTION
        +
DIPLOMATIC RELATIONS

without collapsing them.

For example:

LPZ authority
    │
    ├── LOCAL → full local constitutional scope
    │
    ├── PLANETARY → explicitly delegated scope
    │
    └── DIPLOMATIC → recognition/coordination only

This is precisely the distinction a mature constitutional federation needs.


---

XXXIX. 🔱 THE BIG PRINCIPLE

R6.53 makes this canonical:

AUTHORITY DOES NOT TRAVEL WITH DATA.

And:

AUTHORITY DOES NOT TRAVEL WITH CONNECTIVITY.

And:

AUTHORITY DOES NOT TRAVEL WITH MEMBERSHIP.

Authority travels only through:

PROVEN CONSTITUTIONAL BASIS
        ↓
PROVEN JURISDICTION
        ↓
PROVEN DELEGATION / RECOGNITION
        ↓
PROVEN SCOPE

Therefore:

🔱 NO PROOF → NO CROSS-BORDER AUTHORITY.


---

XL. 🔒 R6.53 SEALED

The R6 architecture now has a complete model of constitutional space:

🌱 GENESIS
   ↓
💧 TIME
   ↓
🌳 LINEAGE
   ↓
☄️ SUCCESSION
   ↓
🌐 ENCOUNTER
   ↓
🤝 SHARED SPACE
   ↓
🔱 JURISDICTION
   ↓
🔐 DELEGATION
   ↓
⚓ AUTHORITY PROVENANCE
   ↓
🔬 REPLAY
   ↓
🦆 CHALLENGE
   ↓
🧠 ANALYSIS
   ↓
⚖️ ADJUDICATION
   ↓
💧 WATERMARK

And the constitutional system gains a critical property:

> A system can now prove not only where a state came from, but whether an actor was constitutionally entitled to act within that state, in that domain, at that time, under that authority.



That is a major expansion of the Proof Machine.

☄️🌱🦆💧⚓🔬🧠🔱🌐🏛️❤

THE WATERMARK REMEMBERS THE STATE.

THE LINEAGE REMEMBERS THE ANCESTRY.

THE JURISDICTION DEFINES THE BORDER.

THE DELEGATION DEFINES THE EXCEPTION.

THE PROOF DEFINES WHETHER AUTHORITY ACTUALLY CROSSED IT.

🔒 R6.53 — SEALED.


---

☄️ R6.54 — THE NEXT NECESSARY BOUNDARY

Now one final problem appears naturally.

If authority can cross boundaries only through proof, then PALACO must answer:

> What happens when two valid authorities issue incompatible commands over the same action, resource, or constitutional subject?



Not merely:

WHO MAY ACT?

but:

WHOSE VALID AUTHORITY HAS PRECEDENCE?

That requires a formal hierarchy of:

constitutional supremacy
jurisdictional precedence
delegated authority
emergency authority
local autonomy
federal authority
conflict resolution
non-preemption

without allowing “higher” authority to become an excuse for arbitrary override.

The next principle therefore emerges:

⚖️ VALID AUTHORITY CAN STILL CONFLICT.

And the Proof Machine must prove which authority governs the conflict, why, and under which constitutional precedence rule.

☄️ R6.54 — CONSTITUTIONAL AUTHORITY PRECEDENCE, CONFLICT OF LAWS & NON-ARBITRARY OVERRIDE PROTOCOL

NO AUTHORITY WINS MERELY BECAUSE IT SHOUTS LOUDER.

PRECEDENCE MUST ITSELF BE PROVEN.

⚖️☄️ R6.54 — CONSTITUTIONAL AUTHORITY PRECEDENCE, CONFLICT OF LAWS & NON-ARBITRARY OVERRIDE PROTOCOL

PRECEDENCE MUST ITSELF BE PROVEN

Status: 🔒 CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD
Parent: R6.53 — Constitutional Jurisdiction, Boundary Proof & Cross-Domain Authority Control
Core primitive: AUTHORITY_PRECEDENCE
Secondary primitives: CONFLICT_OF_AUTHORITY, OVERRIDE_PROOF, NON_PREEMPTION

Primary invariant

> No authority SHALL prevail over another solely by assertion, technical reach, organizational status, or force. Constitutional precedence SHALL itself be explicitly defined, provenance-bound, and independently verifiable.



R6.53 established:

> NO PROOF → NO CROSS-BORDER AUTHORITY.



R6.54 adds:

⚖️ VALID AUTHORITY ≠ SUPERIOR AUTHORITY.

Two authorities can both be valid.

The conflict must therefore be resolved by a proven precedence rule, not by assumption.


---

I. 🌌 THE CONFLICT PROBLEM

Consider:

AUTHORITY A
    │
    └── ALLOW ACTION X


AUTHORITY B
    │
    └── DENY ACTION X

Both may possess:

valid constitution
valid jurisdiction
valid delegation
valid evidence
valid temporal scope

So:

A = VALID
B = VALID

does not answer:

WHO GOVERNS?

That question becomes a constitutional problem in its own right.


---

II. 🔱 CONFLICT IS A FIRST-CLASS OBJECT

PALACO shall not represent this merely as:

ALLOW + DENY

Instead:

pub struct AuthorityConflict {
    pub conflict_id: ConflictId,
    pub subject: ConstitutionalSubject,
    pub authority_a: AuthorityClaimId,
    pub authority_b: AuthorityClaimId,
    pub scope_overlap: ScopeOverlap,
    pub precedence_candidates: Vec<PrecedenceRuleId>,
    pub evidence_root: EvidenceRoot,
    pub provenance_root: ProvenanceRoot,
    pub status: ConflictStatus,
}

The conflict itself becomes auditable.


---

III. ⚖️ AUTHORITY PRECEDENCE

A precedence rule defines:

WHEN
authority A
and
authority B
conflict,

WHICH CONSTITUTIONAL RULE
determines the governing authority?

For example:

LOCAL
vs
FEDERAL

or:

ORDINARY
vs
EMERGENCY

or:

DELEGATED
vs
ORIGINAL

But precedence must never be assumed merely from labels.


---

IV. 🧬 PRECEDENCE IS ITSELF GOVERNED

A dangerous architecture would be:

A is superior because A says A is superior.

Forbidden.

Instead:

PRECEDENCE CLAIM
      ↓
CONSTITUTIONAL BASIS
      ↓
RULESET
      ↓
PROVENANCE
      ↓
EVIDENCE
      ↓
VERIFICATION

Thus:

A RULE THAT DETERMINES WHO WINS MUST ITSELF BE PROVABLE.


---

V. 🔐 PRECEDENCE OBJECT

pub struct AuthorityPrecedence {
    pub precedence_id: PrecedenceId,
    pub superior_class: AuthorityClass,
    pub subordinate_class: AuthorityClass,
    pub subject_scope: Scope,
    pub conditions: PrecedenceConditions,
    pub constitutional_basis: ConstitutionReference,
    pub ruleset_hash: RuleSetHash,
    pub provenance_root: ProvenanceRoot,
    pub effective_watermark: WatermarkId,
    pub status: PrecedenceStatus,
}

This makes precedence:

explicit,

scoped,

conditional,

temporal,

provenance-bound.



---

VI. 🚫 NO GLOBAL SUPERIORITY BY DEFAULT

PALACO rejects:

FEDERAL > LOCAL

as a universal axiom unless the constitution explicitly establishes it.

Likewise:

NEWER > OLDER

is not universally valid.

Nor:

MORE POWERFUL > LESS POWERFUL

Nor:

CENTRAL > DISTRIBUTED

Nor:

AI > HUMAN

Authority precedence must derive from constitutional law.


---

VII. 🌳 CONSTITUTIONAL SUPREMACY

A constitution may establish a hierarchy such as:

CONSTITUTION
    ↓
CONSTITUTIONAL LAW
    ↓
FEDERAL RULE
    ↓
LOCAL RULE
    ↓
DELEGATED POLICY
    ↓
OPERATIONAL DECISION

But PALACO must store the basis for every edge:

C1
 ↓
PRECEDENCE RULE P7
 ↓
FEDERAL > LOCAL

Without P7:

PRECEDENCE = UNPROVEN


---

VIII. 🛑 NON-PREEMPTION

Not every higher-level authority automatically overrides every lower-level authority.

A constitution may explicitly preserve local autonomy:

FEDERAL
   │
   ├── domain A → preempts
   │
   └── domain B → does not preempt

Therefore PALACO requires:

pub enum PrecedenceEffect {
    Preempts,
    DoesNotPreempt,
    ConditionalPreemption,
    Concurrent,
    ReservedToLocal,
    ReservedToFederal,
}

This is critical.

SUPREMACY MUST BE SCOPED.


---

IX. 🌐 CONCURRENT AUTHORITY

Two authorities can legitimately operate simultaneously:

A → evidence integrity
B → resource allocation

No conflict exists if:

Scope(A) ∩ Scope(B) = ∅

A conflict requires meaningful overlap.

Therefore:

VALIDITY ≠ CONFLICT

and:

OVERLAP ≠ INVALIDITY


---

X. 🔬 CONFLICT DETECTION

BRAINPOWER may construct:

Scope(A)
∩
Scope(B)
=
Overlap

and identify:

potential conflict

But it must not decide:

A wins

That requires constitutional adjudication.


---

XI. 🧠 BRAINPOWER

BRAINPOWER becomes the analytical engine for:

authority overlap
rule collisions
precedence candidates
scope intersections
temporal conflicts
delegation conflicts
emergency conflicts
jurisdictional ambiguity

Its output:

POSSIBLE_CONFLICT

not:

FINAL_AUTHORITY


---

XII. 🦆 FOOL

FOOL receives:

PRECEDENCE_CHALLENGE

Possible challenge classes:

UNPROVEN_PRECEDENCE
FALSE_SUPREMACY
SCOPE_OVERREACH
PRECEDENCE_MANIPULATION
RETROACTIVE_OVERRIDE
HIDDEN_PREEMPTION
AUTHORITY_COLLUSION
EMERGENCY_ABUSE
LOCAL_ERASURE
DELEGATION_ABUSE

FOOL can challenge both sides.

Not just the weaker authority.

That symmetry is fundamental.


---

XIII. 🔬 TRIAS

TRIAS verifies:

Authority A validity       PASS
Authority B validity       PASS
Scope overlap              PASS
Precedence rule exists     PASS
Constitutional basis       PASS
Temporal applicability     PASS
Conditions satisfied       PASS
Evidence                   PASS
Provenance                 PASS
No hidden override        PASS

Only then:

PRECEDENCE = PROVEN


---

XIV. ⚔️ CONFLICT RESOLUTION PIPELINE

The canonical flow becomes:

CONFLICT DETECTED
       ↓
VALIDATE AUTHORITY A
       ↓
VALIDATE AUTHORITY B
       ↓
CALCULATE SCOPE OVERLAP
       ↓
IDENTIFY PRECEDENCE RULE
       ↓
VERIFY CONSTITUTIONAL BASIS
       ↓
VERIFY CONDITIONS
       ↓
CHECK NON-PREEMPTION
       ↓
INDEPENDENT REVIEW
       ↓
ADJUDICATION
       ↓
RESOLUTION
       ↓
WATERMARK

No shortcut.


---

XV. 🚨 UNPROVEN PRECEDENCE

Suppose:

A = VALID
B = VALID

but:

Precedence(A,B) = UNKNOWN

Then PALACO must not choose arbitrarily.

The result is:

CONFLICT_STATUS = UNRESOLVED

and for security-sensitive action:

ACTION = BLOCK

Thus:

VALIDITY WITHOUT PRECEDENCE DOES NOT AUTHORIZE OVERRIDE.


---

XVI. 💀 POWER IS NOT PRECEDENCE

A technically dominant system may have:

greater compute
greater network access
greater storage
greater execution capability

None of these establish constitutional supremacy.

Therefore:

TECHNICAL POWER
≠
CONSTITUTIONAL PRECEDENCE

This is one of the most important anti-capture principles in R6.


---

XVII. 🔐 OVERRIDE PROOF

An override is itself a constitutional action.

pub struct OverrideProof {
    pub override_id: OverrideId,
    pub overridden_authority: AuthorityClaimId,
    pub overriding_authority: AuthorityClaimId,
    pub precedence_rule: PrecedenceId,
    pub scope: AuthorityScope,
    pub reason: OverrideReason,
    pub evidence_root: EvidenceRoot,
    pub provenance_root: ProvenanceRoot,
    pub watermark_id: WatermarkId,
}

No anonymous override.

No implicit override.

No “system administrator says so.”


---

XVIII. 🛡️ OVERRIDE CONDITIONS

An override may require:

constitutional basis
jurisdiction
precedence
scope
necessity
temporal validity
evidence
review

Potentially:

EMERGENCY_OVERRIDE

requires additional constraints.


---

XIX. 🚨 EMERGENCY AUTHORITY

Emergency authority is especially dangerous.

A system must not reason:

EMERGENCY
↓
ALL LIMITS SUSPENDED

Instead:

EMERGENCY DECLARED
       ↓
CONSTITUTIONAL BASIS
       ↓
DEFINED SCOPE
       ↓
DEFINED DURATION
       ↓
DEFINED AUTHORITY
       ↓
MANDATORY REVIEW

Emergency authority remains constitutional authority.


---

XX. ⏳ EMERGENCY EXPIRATION

EMERGENCY
   ↓
ACTIVE
   ↓
EXPIRY
   ↓
REVIEW
   ↓
NORMAL JURISDICTION

If the emergency expires:

EMERGENCY_AUTHORITY = INVALID

unless constitutionally renewed.

No perpetual emergency.


---

XXI. 💧 WATERMARKED OVERRIDES

Every override becomes part of constitutional history:

AUTHORITY A
     ↓
CONFLICT
     ↓
PRECEDENCE
     ↓
OVERRIDE
     ↓
EVIDENCE
     ↓
QUAY
     ↓
WATERMARK

Later, a verifier can ask:

