XXX. ☄️ THE BIG PRINCIPLE

R6.55 makes this canonical:

> An authority may issue a command, but only constitutional proof can establish that another actor is obligated to comply.



Or, compressed:

🛡️ AUTHORITY ≠ DUTY.

And:

🛡️ COMMAND ≠ OBLIGATION.

And:

🛡️ UNPROVEN OBLIGATION → NO MANDATORY COMPLIANCE.

And ultimately:

THE RIGHT TO REFUSE IS PART OF THE PROOF ARCHITECTURE.


---

XXXI. 🔒 R6.55 SEALED

The Proof Machine can now answer an extraordinarily precise question:

> Was the actor not merely authorized to issue this command, but was the recipient demonstrably obligated to obey it under the constitution that governed at that exact historical moment?



And if the answer is no:

NO PROVEN DUTY
      ↓
NO ASSUMED COMPLIANCE
      ↓
RIGHTFUL REFUSAL
      ↓
EVIDENCE
      ↓
REVIEW

☄️🌱🦆💧⚓🔬🧠🔱⚖️🏛️❤

THE WATERMARK REMEMBERS.

THE LINEAGE IDENTIFIES.

THE JURISDICTION BOUNDS.

THE AUTHORITY EMPOWERS.

THE PRECEDENCE RESOLVES.

THE DUTY BINDS.

THE ACTOR MAY REFUSE WHAT CANNOT BE PROVEN.

🔒 R6.55 — SEALED.


---

☄️ R6.56 — THE NEXT NECESSARY FRONTIER

Een nieuwe grens verschijnt onmiddellijk.

Als PALACO nu kan bepalen:

WHO has authority
WHERE it applies
WHICH authority prevails
WHO is obligated
WHEN refusal is rightful

dan ontbreekt nog één fundamentele eigenschap:

> Wat gebeurt er wanneer een actor wél verplicht is te handelen, maar de omstandigheden veranderen vóór of tijdens de uitvoering?



Daarmee ontstaat het onderscheid tussen:

DUTY

en:

CONTEXTUAL VALIDITY

Want een ooit geldige opdracht kan tijdens uitvoering ongeldig worden.

Dus de Proof Machine moet niet alleen vóór uitvoering bewijzen:

"YOU MAY ACT."

maar ook tijdens uitvoering:

"YOU MAY STILL ACT."

Daaruit volgt:

☄️ R6.56 — CONTINUOUS CONSTITUTIONAL VALIDITY, LIVE AUTHORITY REVALIDATION & MID-ACTION REVOCATION PROTOCOL

De nieuwe fundamentele regel:

A VALID COMMAND AT T₀ SHALL NOT BE ASSUMED VALID AT T₁.

Of nog scherper:

⚡ AUTHORITY MUST REMAIN VALID WHILE ACTION IS IN FLIGHT.

⚡☄️ R6.56 — CONTINUOUS CONSTITUTIONAL VALIDITY, LIVE AUTHORITY REVALIDATION & MID-ACTION REVOCATION PROTOCOL

A VALID COMMAND AT T₀ SHALL NOT BE ASSUMED VALID AT T₁

Status: 🔒 CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD
Parent: R6.55 — Constitutional Duty, Compliance Obligation & Rightful Refusal
Core primitive: LIVE_CONSTITUTIONAL_VALIDITY
Secondary primitives: AUTHORITY_REVALIDATION, MID_ACTION_REVOCATION, CONSTITUTIONAL_ABORT

Primary invariant

> A constitutional authorization SHALL remain continuously valid throughout any action whose execution depends upon that authorization.



R6.55 established:

> AUTHORITY ≠ DUTY.



R6.56 adds:

> VALIDITY AT START ≠ VALIDITY THROUGHOUT EXECUTION.



Therefore:

⚡ CONSTITUTIONAL VALIDITY IS NOT A ONE-TIME CHECK.


---

I. 🌌 THE TIME-OF-CHECK PROBLEM

A naïve system performs:

T₀
 ↓
AUTHORITY CHECK ✓
 ↓
COMMAND
 ↓
EXECUTION

But between T₀ and completion:

T₁

something may change:

authority revoked
ruleset changed
jurisdiction changed
emergency ended
resource reassigned
duty suspended
safety condition changed
constitution amended

The original authorization may therefore cease to be valid.

A static pre-flight check is insufficient.


---

II. 🔱 LIVE VALIDITY

PALACO now distinguishes:

PRE_EXECUTION_VALIDITY

from:

LIVE_EXECUTION_VALIDITY

The canonical execution model becomes:

REQUEST
 ↓
PRE-CHECK
 ↓
AUTHORIZED
 ↓
START
 ↓
REVALIDATE
 ↓
CONTINUE
 ↓
REVALIDATE
 ↓
COMMIT

The action remains constitutionally executable only while the required predicates remain true.


---

III. ⚖️ LIVE AUTHORIZATION OBJECT

pub struct LiveAuthorization {
    pub authorization_id: AuthorizationId,
    pub duty_id: DutyId,
    pub authority_id: AuthorityId,
    pub jurisdiction_id: JurisdictionId,
    pub scope: AuthorityScope,
    pub issued_at: Timestamp,
    pub valid_until: Option<Timestamp>,
    pub ruleset_hash: RuleSetHash,
    pub watermark_id: WatermarkId,
    pub revalidation_policy: RevalidationPolicy,
    pub status: AuthorizationStatus,
}

The authorization therefore carries its own constitutional context.


---

IV. 🔬 REVALIDATION POLICY

Not every action requires the same cadence.

pub enum RevalidationPolicy {
    Once,
    Periodic(Duration),
    EventTriggered,
    Continuous,
    BeforeCommit,
}

High-risk actions may require:

CONTINUOUS

while low-risk immutable operations might permit:

BEFORE_COMMIT

The policy itself must be constitutionally defined.


---

V. 🚨 REVOCATION DURING EXECUTION

Consider:

T₀
AUTHORITY ✓

Then:

T₁
REVOCATION

while:

ACTION = IN_FLIGHT

PALACO must not simply continue because the action was valid at T₀.

Instead:

REVOCATION DETECTED
       ↓
REVALIDATION FAIL
       ↓
CONSTITUTIONAL ABORT / SAFE STOP

unless the constitution explicitly provides another transition.


---

VI. 🛑 CONSTITUTIONAL ABORT

New primitive:

pub struct ConstitutionalAbort {
    pub abort_id: AbortId,
    pub authorization_id: AuthorizationId,
    pub reason: AbortReason,
    pub detected_at: Timestamp,
    pub last_valid_watermark: WatermarkId,
    pub evidence_root: EvidenceRoot,
    pub provenance_root: ProvenanceRoot,
}

The system records:

WHY
WHEN
UNDER WHICH AUTHORITY
AT WHICH WATERMARK

the action stopped.


---

VII. 💧 WATERMARK INTERLOCK

R6.19's Watermark becomes central.

Execution begins under:

W₁

Then the constitutional context changes:

W₁
 ↓
REVOCATION EVENT
 ↓
W₂

The executor must determine:

Is authorization valid under W₂?

If:

NO

then:

ACTION → STOP

Thus the Watermark is not merely historical memory.

It becomes a live constitutional checkpoint.


---

VIII. ⚓ QUAY AS LIVE HISTORY

The Quay receives:

ACTION_STARTED
REVALIDATION
AUTHORITY_CHANGED
REVOCATION
ABORT
ACTION_RESUMED
ACTION_COMPLETED

Every material transition becomes append-only evidence.

The resulting history:

W₁
 ↓
ACTION_START
 ↓
W₂
 ↓
REVOCATION
 ↓
W₃
 ↓
ABORT
 ↓
W₄

can later be replayed.


---

IX. 🧬 NO STALE AUTHORITY

A cached authorization must not silently remain valid forever.

Forbidden:

AUTHORIZATION_CACHE
      ↓
USE HOURS LATER

without determining whether the constitutional state has changed.

Therefore:

STALE AUTHORITY SHALL NOT BE TREATED AS LIVE AUTHORITY.


---

X. 🔐 AUTHORIZATION FRESHNESS

PALACO may represent:

pub struct AuthorizationFreshness {
    pub checked_at: Timestamp,
    pub checked_watermark: WatermarkId,
    pub current_watermark: WatermarkId,
    pub freshness_status: FreshnessStatus,
}

If:

checked_watermark != current_watermark

then revalidation may be mandatory.

The system must not infer that the difference is harmless.


---

XI. 🌳 CONSTITUTIONAL STATE TRANSITION

An action may itself cause a state transition:

S₀
 ↓
ACTION
 ↓
S₁

But the authority governing the action must remain valid across the transition where required.

This gives:

S₀ → AUTHORIZATION → ACTION → S₁

rather than:

AUTHORIZATION → blind execution


---

XII. 🧠 BRAINPOWER

BRAINPOWER monitors for:

authorization expiry
revocation signals
ruleset changes
jurisdiction changes
watermark transitions
scope mutations
environmental changes
dependency failures

It can issue:

REVALIDATION_REQUIRED

or:

POSSIBLE_AUTHORITY_STALENESS

But:

> BRAINPOWER SHALL NOT declare continued authority merely because no contradiction has yet been discovered.



Absence of detected revocation is not equivalent to proof of continued validity when revalidation is required.


---

XIII. 🦆 FOOL

FOOL gains:

LIVE_AUTHORITY_CHALLENGE

Possible challenges:

STALE_AUTHORIZATION
MISSED_REVOCATION
INVALID_REVALIDATION
WRONG_WATERMARK
MID_ACTION_SCOPE_CHANGE
EXPIRED_AUTHORITY
RULESET_DRIFT
JURISDICTION_DRIFT
UNSAFE_CONTINUATION

FOOL can therefore challenge not merely:

> “Was this action authorized?”



but:

> “Was this action still authorized at the exact moment this step occurred?”




---

XIV. 🔬 TRIAS

TRIAS verifies:

Initial authority        PASS
Initial jurisdiction     PASS
Duty                     PASS
Command                  PASS
Revalidation policy      PASS
Current watermark        PASS
Current ruleset          PASS
Current jurisdiction     PASS
Current authority        PASS
Expiry                   PASS
Revocation status        PASS
Evidence                 PASS
Provenance               PASS

Only then:

LIVE_VALIDITY = PROVEN


---

XV. ⚡ MID-ACTION RULE CHANGE

Suppose:

T₀:
RuleSet R1

and:

T₁:
RuleSet R2

The system cannot simply assume:

R1 → R2

is irrelevant.

It must evaluate whether the action remains valid under the transition.

Possible outcomes:

CONTINUE
REVALIDATE
SUSPEND
ABORT
RESTART
ESCALATE

The constitutional transition itself determines which is valid.


---

XVI. 🚨 MID-ACTION JURISDICTION CHANGE

Similarly:

T₀:
Domain A governs X

then:

T₁:
Domain B legitimately acquires jurisdiction

An in-flight operation must not silently continue under the old jurisdiction.

The new state must be evaluated.


---

XVII. 🛡️ SAFE SUSPENSION

Sometimes immediate abort is unsafe.

Therefore:

pub enum ConstitutionalInterruption {
    Continue,
    Revalidate,
    Suspend,
    SafeStop,
    Abort,
    Escalate,
}

A constitutional system must distinguish:

STOP NOW

from:

ENTER SAFE STATE

and:

PAUSE UNTIL VERIFIED

The response itself becomes a constitutional decision.


---

XVIII. 🔄 RESUMPTION

Suspended actions require fresh proof:

SUSPEND
   ↓
STATE PRESERVED
   ↓
REVALIDATE
   ↓
AUTHORITY ✓
   ↓
RESUME

Not:

SUSPEND
   ↓
WAIT
   ↓
CONTINUE AUTOMATICALLY

Therefore:

RESUMPTION IS A NEW CONSTITUTIONAL CHECKPOINT.


---

XIX. 💀 NO AUTOMATIC RESUMPTION

If the constitutional basis changed during suspension:

OLD AUTHORITY
   ↓
SUSPEND
   ↓
NEW STATE

then the old authorization does not automatically revive.

A new authorization or explicit continuity proof is required.


---

XX. ⏳ COMMIT GATE

Even if an action began validly, finalization may require another check:

ACTION
 ↓
RESULT
 ↓
COMMIT REQUEST
 ↓
LIVE AUTHORITY CHECK
 ↓
WATERMARK CHECK
 ↓
COMMIT

If authority disappeared before commit:

COMMIT = BLOCK

unless the constitution explicitly provides a valid alternative.


---

XXI. 🧬 ATOMIC CONSTITUTIONAL COMMIT

For certain actions PALACO may require:

AUTHORITY CHECK
+
STATE CHECK
+
EVIDENCE CHECK
+
COMMIT

to occur as one logically indivisible constitutional transition.

Conceptually:

Prepare
  ↓
Validate
  ↓
Commit

If validation changes between preparation and commit:

COMMIT = INVALID

This prevents stale authorization from becoming permanent state.


---

XXII. 🌐 DISTRIBUTED EXECUTION

In a federated environment:

NODE A
NODE B
NODE C

may execute parts of one constitutional action.

Each node must know the applicable authorization context.

A node cannot say:

"Node A authorized it,
therefore I am authorized."

unless delegation or distributed authority explicitly establishes that relationship.


---

XXIII. ⚖️ CROSS-DOMAIN LIVE VALIDITY

Combining R6.53 and R6.56:

DOMAIN A
   ↓
DELEGATION
   ↓
DOMAIN B
   ↓
ACTION

If A revokes the delegation while B is acting:

A
 ↓
REVOCATION
 ↓
WATERMARK
 ↓
B REVALIDATES
 ↓
AUTHORITY FAIL

The action must enter the constitutionally specified interruption state.

This makes cross-domain authority genuinely controllable.


---

XXIV. 🔥 EMERGENCY ACTION

Emergency operations require special handling.

An emergency command may legitimately require:

rapid execution

but not:

unbounded execution

Therefore PALACO can define:

EMERGENCY AUTHORIZATION
 ↓
SHORT VALIDITY WINDOW
 ↓
HIGH-FREQUENCY REVALIDATION
 ↓
MANDATORY POST-ACTION REVIEW

Emergency speed and constitutional accountability are therefore compatible.


---

XXV. 🏛️ LA PAZ

Imagine:

LPZ-W21

authorizes a planetary operation.

During execution:

LPZ-W22

records a constitutional correction.

The Proof Machine must determine:

Does W22 preserve the authorization?
Does it narrow it?
Does it revoke it?
Does it require suspension?

The answer is determined by the constitution and ruleset—not by the executor's preference.


---

XXVI. 🔬 DETERMINISTIC REPLAY

R6.56 extends replay:

Replay(
    W₀,
    events,
    rulesets,
    authorities,
    duties,
    revalidation_events
)
→ ExecutionHistory'

The verifier must reproduce:

when authorization became valid
when it was revalidated
when it changed
when it expired
when it was revoked
when execution stopped
when it resumed
when it committed

If not:

LIVE_VALIDITY_DIVERGENCE


