☄️🌱🦆 GO.

☄️ R6.61 — CONSTITUTIONAL SOVEREIGNTY, CROSS-LINEAGE RECOGNITION & DIPLOMATIC TRUST PROTOCOL

RECOGNITION WITHOUT SUBORDINATION

Status: 🔒 CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD
Parent: R6.60 — Constitutional Lineage Status, Succession Authority & Canonical Present Determination
Core primitive: SOVEREIGN_DOMAIN
Supporting primitives: RECOGNITION, TRUST_ASSERTION, DIPLOMATIC_RELATION, AUTHORITY_SCOPE, FEDERATION_RELATION

Primary invariant

> Recognition of another constitutional domain SHALL NOT, by itself, confer authority over that domain, merge its lineage, or alter its canonical present.



R6.60 established:

> THE PRESENT MUST PROVE WHY IT IS THE PRESENT.



R6.61 establishes:

🌐 A CONSTITUTION MAY RECOGNIZE ANOTHER CONSTITUTION WITHOUT BECOMING THAT CONSTITUTION.


---

I. 🌌 THE NEXT PROBLEM

Consider:

PLANETARY CONSTITUTION
                           │
              ┌────────────┴────────────┐
              ▼                         ▼
          CITADEL A                 CITADEL B
          L-A / W-A                 L-B / W-B

Both have:

their own lineage
their own Watermarks
their own authority
their own canonical present
their own Quay
their own constitutional history

Neither is subordinate.

Yet they may need to communicate.

The question becomes:

> How can one constitutional system recognize the validity of another without accidentally claiming jurisdiction over it?




---

II. 🔱 SOVEREIGN DOMAIN

Introduce:

pub struct SovereignDomain {
    pub domain_id: DomainId,
    pub lineage_id: LineageId,
    pub canonical_watermark: WatermarkId,
    pub constitution_hash: ConstitutionHash,
    pub authority_scope: AuthorityScope,
    pub recognition_policy: RecognitionPolicy,
}

A domain therefore possesses a bounded constitutional identity.


---

III. 🏛️ SOVEREIGNTY

PALACO sovereignty does not mean unrestricted power.

It means:

AUTHORITY WITHIN DEFINED CONSTITUTIONAL SCOPE

Therefore:

A-SOVEREIGNTY

does not imply:

A-AUTHORITY-OVER-B

unless B explicitly delegates or federates that authority.


---

IV. 🚫 NO IMPLICIT JURISDICTION

Forbidden:

A recognizes B
      ↓
A controls B

Recognition creates:

A knows B exists

not:

A governs B

Canonical rule:

RECOGNITION ≠ JURISDICTION.


---

V. 🌐 RECOGNITION OBJECT

pub struct RecognitionRecord {
    pub recognition_id: RecognitionId,
    pub recognizing_domain: DomainId,
    pub recognized_domain: DomainId,
    pub recognized_watermark: WatermarkId,
    pub recognized_lineage: LineageId,
    pub recognition_scope: RecognitionScope,
    pub evidence_root: EvidenceRoot,
    pub effective_at: Timestamp,
    pub expires_at: Option<Timestamp>,
}

Recognition itself therefore becomes a constitutional fact.


---

VI. 🧬 RECOGNITION IS VERSIONED

A domain does not merely recognize:

B

It recognizes:

B at Watermark W₁

This matters because B may evolve:

B-W1
 ↓
B-W2
 ↓
B-W3

Recognition of B-W1 must not silently imply recognition of every future state.

Therefore:

RECOGNITION IS WATERMARK-BOUND.


---

VII. 💧 RECOGNITION CHAIN

Example:

A
│
├── recognizes B-W10
│
└── recognizes B-W12

The second recognition may supersede the first:

B-W10 → B-W12

but B-W10 remains historically recognized.

The record is never rewritten.


---

VIII. ⚖️ RECOGNITION LEVELS

PALACO can distinguish:

OBSERVED
IDENTIFIED
AUTHENTICATED
RECOGNIZED
TRUSTED
COOPERATIVE
FEDERATED

These are not equivalent.

For example:

OBSERVED

means:

> We have evidence this domain exists.



While:

RECOGNIZED

means:

> We accept this domain as the identified constitutional entity represented by the supplied proof.



And:

FEDERATED

means:

> A formal constitutional relationship exists between the domains.




---

IX. 🔐 TRUST IS NOT RECOGNITION

A domain can recognize another without trusting every action it performs.

Therefore:

RECOGNITION(B)

does not automatically mean:

TRUST(EVERYTHING B DOES)

Trust must be scoped.


---

