Evolution:

M₀ → M₁ → M₂ → M₃

No semantic generation may appear without an explainable predecessor unless explicitly declared as genesis.


---

III. 🔬 SEMANTIC DIFF

Before migration:

M₁
 ↓
SEMANTIC DIFF
 ↓
M₂

The diff must identify:

added meanings
removed meanings
narrowed meanings
broadened meanings
changed constraints
changed authority implications
changed temporal semantics
changed interpretation rules

Conceptually:

pub struct SemanticDiff {
    pub added: Vec<SemanticElement>,
    pub removed: Vec<SemanticElement>,
    pub modified: Vec<SemanticChange>,
    pub authority_impact: AuthorityImpact,
    pub compatibility: CompatibilityClass,
}


---

IV. 🚨 SILENT MEANING EXPANSION

Suppose:

V1:
"Agent may act within scope X."

becomes:

V2:
"Agent may act."

The scope restriction disappeared.

That is not a harmless wording change.

It is:

SEMANTIC_BROADENING
+
AUTHORITY_IMPACT

and therefore requires constitutional review.


---

V. 🔱 SEMANTIC CHANGE CLASSES

R6.64 defines:

IDENTICAL
CLARIFICATION
REFINEMENT
NARROWING
BROADENING
EXTENSION
RESTRICTION
CONTEXTUALIZATION
INCOMPATIBLE
BREAKING

The classification itself becomes evidence.


---

VI. 🌱 CLARIFICATION

Example:

V1:
"Emergency action requires authorization."

V2:
"Emergency action requires authorization from an authority holding
the emergency-response mandate."

If V2 merely makes an existing requirement explicit:

CHANGE = CLARIFICATION

But this claim itself must be proven.

The system cannot simply label every change a clarification to avoid migration requirements.


---

VII. 🌳 REFINEMENT

A refinement adds precision without changing the intended constitutional boundary.

V1:
"Evidence must be valid."

V2:
"Evidence must have valid provenance, integrity, attribution,
and applicable temporal scope."

Potentially:

REFINEMENT

provided equivalence is demonstrable.


---

VIII. ⚖️ NARROWING

V1:
Agent may perform X.

V2:
Agent may perform X only under condition Y.

This is:

SEMANTIC_NARROWING

and potentially:

AUTHORITY_REDUCTION

Such a migration is generally safer than silent broadening, but it remains constitutionally material.


---

IX. 🔥 BROADENING

V1:
Agent may perform X.

V2:
Agent may perform X and Y.

This is:

SEMANTIC_BROADENING

and therefore requires explicit authority analysis.

SEMANTIC BROADENING SHALL NEVER BE IMPLIED BY COMPATIBILITY.


---

X. 💀 BREAKING SEMANTIC CHANGE

If:

meaning(V1) ∩ meaning(V2)

is insufficient to preserve previous constitutional interpretation, then:

COMPATIBILITY = BREAKING

A breaking semantic migration cannot masquerade as a version bump with ordinary compatibility.


---

XI. 🔐 COMPATIBILITY PROOF

A semantic migration requires:

OLD MEANING
    ↓
SEMANTIC DIFF
    ↓
IMPACT ANALYSIS
    ↓
COMPATIBILITY TEST
    ↓
COMPATIBILITY PROOF

Conceptually:

pub struct CompatibilityProof {
    pub proof_id: CompatibilityProofId,
    pub predecessor: MeaningId,
    pub successor: MeaningId,
    pub diff: SemanticDiffId,
    pub preserved_properties: Vec<PropertyId>,
    pub changed_properties: Vec<PropertyId>,
    pub authority_impact: AuthorityImpact,
    pub result: CompatibilityResult,
}


---

XII. 🧭 COMPATIBILITY RESULTS

Compatible
ConditionallyCompatible
BackwardCompatible
ForwardCompatible
NonCompatible
Breaking
Unknown

Unknown is important.

It means:

> We do not have enough proof to claim compatibility.



Not:

> probably compatible.




---

XIII. 🚫 UNKNOWN ≠ COMPATIBLE

Canonical rule:

UNKNOWN
   ≠
COMPATIBLE

and:

UNTESTED
   ≠
SAFE

This follows the central epistemic architecture of PALACO.


---

XIV. 🧠 BRAINPOWER

BRAINPOWER may simulate:

semantic consequences
historical interpretation
policy impacts
authority changes
dependency effects
backward compatibility
future compatibility

It can generate:

MIGRATION_HYPOTHESIS

but not:

MIGRATION_PROOF

The distinction remains absolute.


---

XV. 🦆 FOOL

FOOL gains the ability to challenge evolution itself.

New challenges:

SEMANTIC_MIGRATION_CHALLENGE
COMPATIBILITY_CHALLENGE
HIDDEN_BROADENING
HIDDEN_NARROWING
RETROACTIVE_REINTERPRETATION
MISSING_PREDECESSOR
UNPROVEN_EQUIVALENCE
UNDECLARED_AUTHORITY_IMPACT

FOOL may ask:

"What exactly changed?"

and then:

"What proves that the change did not alter the historical meaning?"


---

XVI. 🔬 TRIAS

TRIAS validates:

predecessor
semantic diff
definition hashes
scope
ruleset
authority impact
compatibility proof
migration evidence
effective Watermark
replay result

TRIAS must independently establish:

M_old
  ↓
defined transformation
  ↓
M_new

rather than trusting the migration author's declaration.


---

XVII. ⚓ QUAY

The Quay preserves:

M₀
M₁
M₂
...

together with:

semantic diffs
migration proposals
proofs
challenges
adjudications
effective Watermarks
replay results

Thus semantic evolution becomes append-only constitutional history.


---

XVIII. 💧 MIGRATION WATERMARK

Every successful migration receives a Watermark:

M₁
 ↓
Migration Event
 ↓
Proof
 ↓
M₂
 ↓
W₂

The Watermark binds:

new meaning
migration event
evidence
ruleset
provenance
time
predecessor

Therefore the semantic transition itself becomes historically anchored.


---

XIX. 🕰️ EFFECTIVE TIME

A new meaning may be:

PROPOSED

long before it becomes:

ACTIVE

Therefore:

proposal_time
verification_time
approval_time
effective_time

must remain distinct.

No future meaning may silently govern a past event.


---

XX. 🚫 RETROACTIVE REINTERPRETATION

Suppose:

Event E occurred under M₁.

Later:

M₂

changes the meaning of the relevant term.

PALACO must replay E using:

M₁

not:

M₂

unless a separate constitutional mechanism explicitly establishes retroactive applicability.

And even then, the retroactive transformation must itself be evidenced.


---

XXI. 🔥 THE HISTORICAL INTERPRETATION RULE

Canonical:

> Historical events SHALL be interpreted according to the semantic state applicable to the event unless an explicitly authorized, independently proven retroactive rule states otherwise.



This protects the integrity of the historical record.


---

XXII. 🧬 SEMANTIC MIGRATION GRAPH

The complete model:

M₀
 │
 ▼
SEMANTIC DIFF
 │
 ▼
IMPACT ANALYSIS
 │
 ▼
