---

I. 🌌 THE RE-DIVERGENCE PROBLEM

After a proven synthesis:

G₁ ─────┐
        │
G₂ ─────┼──→ G₃
        │
G₇ ─────┘

the resulting generation may later divide:

G₃
                  │
             RE-DIVERGENCE
              /         \
             ▼           ▼
           G₄A          G₄B

The naïve model says:

G₃ → G₄A
G₃ → G₄B

But constitutionally, that is incomplete.

The system must also prove:

What did A inherit?
What did B inherit?
What did A relinquish?
What did B relinquish?
Which obligations remain shared?
Which authorities became distinct?
Which meanings diverged?

Therefore:

A SPLIT IS A CONSTITUTIONAL EVENT.

Not merely a version increment.


---

II. 🔱 SPLIT OBJECT

pub struct ConstitutionalSplit {
    pub split_id: SplitId,
    pub source_generation: GenerationId,
    pub source_watermark: WatermarkId,
    pub descendants: Vec<GenerationId>,
    pub partition_proof: PartitionProofId,
    pub guarantee_partition: GuaranteePartitionId,
    pub authority_partition: AuthorityPartitionId,
    pub semantic_partition: SemanticPartitionId,
    pub split_event: EventId,
}

The split itself becomes evidence-bearing.


---

III. 💧 COMMON PARENT ANCHOR

Both descendants must identify:

G₃
W₃

as their common constitutional predecessor.

Thus:

W₃
 │
 ├── W₄A
 │
 └── W₄B

The historical relationship is explicit.


---

IV. 🌳 PARTITION PROOF

The partition proof answers:

What existed before the split?
What is being transferred to A?
What is being transferred to B?
What remains shared?
What is newly created?
What is retired?
What is intentionally excluded?

This produces:

PARTITION_PROOF
├── inherited
├── allocated_A
├── allocated_B
├── shared
├── transformed
├── retired
└── newly_created

Nothing important may simply disappear.


---

V. 🔱 GUARANTEE PARTITION

Suppose G₃ contains:

Guarantees:
A
B
C
D
E

After the split:

G₄A → A B C
G₄B → A D E

The system must establish the disposition of every guarantee:

A → shared
B → A
C → A
D → B
E → B

If a guarantee cannot be mapped:

Guarantee X → UNPROVEN

and the corresponding descendant cannot claim complete constitutional inheritance.


---

VI. 🚫 NO SILENT GUARANTEE LOSS

Forbidden:

G₃:
A B C D E

G₄A:
A B

G₄B:
D E

without explaining:

Where did C go?

The answer cannot simply be:

UNKNOWN

while both descendants claim complete inheritance.

Instead:

C → RETIRED

or:

C → UNRESOLVED

or another explicitly proven disposition.


---

VII. 🌱 GUARANTEE CONSERVATION LEDGER

R6.68 extends the guarantee ledger into a partition ledger:

pub struct GuaranteePartition {
    pub source_guarantee: GuaranteeId,
    pub disposition: GuaranteeDisposition,
    pub destination: DestinationSet,
    pub proof: ProofId,
}

Every inherited guarantee obtains a traceable destination.


---

VIII. ⚖️ AUTHORITY PARTITION

Authorities are more dangerous than ordinary guarantees.

Suppose:

G₃:
Authority X

After divergence:

G₄A → X
G₄B → Y

The system must prove how:

Y

came into existence.

It cannot be silently inherited.


---

IX. 🔐 AUTHORITY CONSERVATION

The split engine must classify authority as:

INHERITED
PARTITIONED
DELEGATED
RESTRICTED
REVOKED
NEW
SHARED

Critical invariant:

> A split SHALL NOT create authority merely by distributing inherited authority across descendants.



Any genuinely new capability requires explicit constitutional authorization.


---

