Supporting primitives: AUTHORITY_ROOT, CAPABILITY_ID, POWER_GRANT, POWER_TRANSFER, AUTHORITY_WATERMARK

Primary invariant

> No constitutional capability SHALL become exercisable by a subject unless its authority, scope, origin, transfer basis, temporal validity, and provenance are independently verifiable.



R6.70 established:

🔱 IDENTITY MUST BE PROVEN.

R6.71 establishes:

⚡ POWER MUST BE PROVEN TOO.


---

I. 🌌 THE NEXT PROBLEM

An entity may be legitimate:

ENTITY A ✓
IDENTITY A ✓

but that does not establish that it possesses every capability historically associated with A.

Consider:

A
│
├── CAPABILITY X
├── CAPABILITY Y
└── CAPABILITY Z
       ↓
   TRANSFORMATION
      /   \
     B     C

Which powers move?

X → B
Y → C
Z → neither

Or perhaps:

X → B+C

Every possibility requires proof.

Therefore:

ENTITY CONTINUITY ≠ AUTHORITY CONTINUITY

and:

AUTHORITY CONTINUITY ≠ CAPABILITY CONTINUITY


---

II. ⚡ CAPABILITY AS A FIRST-CLASS OBJECT

pub struct ConstitutionalCapability {
    pub capability_id: CapabilityId,
    pub authority_root: AuthorityRoot,
    pub origin_watermark: WatermarkId,
    pub scope: CapabilityScope,
    pub constraints: CapabilityConstraints,
    pub holder: SubjectId,
    pub provenance_root: ProvenanceRoot,
    pub status: CapabilityStatus,
}

A capability is therefore not merely:

permission = true

It is a constitutional object with history.


---

III. 🔐 AUTHORITY ROOT

The authority root establishes:

CONSTITUTION
    ↓
RULESET
    ↓
AUTHORITY
    ↓
CAPABILITY

Conceptually:

AuthorityRoot =
H(
    ConstitutionalBasis
    +
RulesetHash
    +
GrantEvent
    +
Scope
    +
Constraints
)

No authority may appear from nowhere.


---

IV. 🌳 CAPABILITY LINEAGE

A capability possesses its own lineage:

C₀
 ↓
C₁
 ↓
C₂
 ↓
C₃

Each transition must explain:

grant
restriction
extension
delegation
transfer
partition
revocation
expiration

Thus PALACO can distinguish:

CAPABILITY_ORIGIN
CAPABILITY_EVOLUTION
CAPABILITY_TRANSFER
CAPABILITY_REVOCATION


---

V. 🚫 NO POWER BY ASSOCIATION

Forbidden:

A has capability X
B becomes successor of A
therefore B has X

Correct:

A has X
↓
identity transition
↓
authority analysis
↓
capability transfer rule
↓
evidence
↓
verification
↓
B may exercise X

This closes a major privilege-escalation vector.


---

VI. 🔱 POWER GRANT

Every new capability requires a grant event:

pub struct PowerGrant {
    pub grant_id: GrantId,
    pub authority_root: AuthorityRoot,
    pub recipient: SubjectId,
    pub capability: CapabilityId,
    pub scope: CapabilityScope,
    pub constraints: CapabilityConstraints,
    pub evidence: EvidenceRef,
    pub proof: ProofId,
}

The grant itself becomes auditable.


---

VII. 💧 POWER TRANSFER

Transfer is distinct from grant.

A owns X
   ↓
TRANSFER
   ↓
B owns X

A transfer must identify:

source
recipient
authority
scope
effective time
transfer basis
evidence
verification

A transfer cannot silently enlarge scope.


---

VIII. 🧬 SCOPE CONSERVATION

This becomes a core invariant:

> A capability transfer SHALL NOT increase the constitutional scope of the transferred capability unless an independent authority grant explicitly authorizes that increase.



Example:

A:
WRITE /vault/a

may become:

B:
WRITE /vault/a

but not automatically:

B:
WRITE /vault/*

Therefore:

TRANSFER ≠ ESCALATION


---

IX. 🔥 CAPABILITY PARTITION

A capability may be partitioned:

CAPABILITY X
│
├── X₁ → B
└── X₂ → C

For example:

X = administer federation

could become:

B = administer domain A
C = administer domain B

The partition must preserve constitutional constraints.


---

X. 🌱 CAPABILITY DELEGATION

Delegation creates another relationship:

A
│
└── delegates X
        ↓
        B

Critically:

B

does not automatically become the owner of:

X

Delegation may instead establish:

DELEGATED_AUTHORITY

with:

maximum scope
duration
revocation
conditions
delegator
delegate


---

XI. 🚫 DELEGATION CANNOT EXCEED GRANT

Canonical rule:

DelegateScope ⊆ HolderScope

unless a separate constitutional grant establishes otherwise.

Thus:

A SUBJECT CANNOT DELEGATE A POWER IT DOES NOT POSSESS.

And:

A DELEGATE CANNOT REDELEGATE MORE POWER THAN IT RECEIVED.


---

XII. 🔁 REDELEGATION

If:

A → B → C

then PALACO records:

A
 ↓ delegation₁
B
 ↓ delegation₂
C

The complete chain must be replayable.

If B attempts:

B → C

with a scope greater than B received:

DELEGATION_ESCALATION

and the action is blocked.


---

XIII. ⚖️ AUTHORITY ≠ CAPABILITY

R6.71 explicitly separates:

AUTHORITY

from:

CAPABILITY

Authority may establish that an actor may possess a class of powers.

Capability establishes the concrete executable permission.

Example:

Authority:
"may administer evidence infrastructure"

Capability:
"append evidence to Quay Q-7 under policy P"

The latter requires a concrete binding.


---

XIV. 🧠 BRAINPOWER

BRAINPOWER may discover:

possible authority inheritance
possible capability transfer
scope mismatch
delegation anomaly
privilege expansion
revocation conflict

It may produce:

CAPABILITY_TRANSFER_CANDIDATE

but never:

CAPABILITY_TRANSFER_CONFIRMED

without proof.


---

XV. 🦆 FOOL

FOOL gains:

CAPABILITY_CHALLENGE
AUTHORITY_CHALLENGE
TRANSFER_CHALLENGE
DELEGATION_CHALLENGE

Possible grounds:

MISSING_GRANT
INVALID_TRANSFER
SCOPE_EXPANSION
UNAUTHORIZED_DELEGATION
REDELEGATION_ESCALATION
EXPIRED_AUTHORITY
BROKEN_PROVENANCE
WRONG_SUBJECT
REVOCATION_CONFLICT
FABRICATED_AUTHORITY

The constitutional question becomes:

> "Show the proof that this subject possesses this exact power, at this exact scope, at this exact time."




---

XVI. 🔬 TRIAS

TRIAS verifies:

authority root
grant
subject
capability
scope
constraints
transfer
delegation
temporal validity
provenance
Watermark
replay

TRIAS must explicitly test:

GrantedScope >= ExercisedScope

If:

ExercisedScope > GrantedScope

then:

PRIVILEGE_ESCALATION


---

XVII. 💀 CAPABILITY GAPS

Suppose:

A has X
A → B

but no proof establishes:

B has X

Then:

CAPABILITY_STATUS = UNPROVEN

Not:

INHERITED

Not:

ASSUMED

Not:

PROBABLY_ALLOWED


---

XVIII. 🕰️ TEMPORAL AUTHORITY

Capabilities have temporal validity:

C₁
│
├── valid_from
└── valid_until

Therefore:

CapabilityActive(C, T)

must be evaluated against:

grant
revocation
expiration
suspension
constitutional generation

A capability that was valid yesterday does not automatically remain valid today.


---

XIX. 💧 AUTHORITY WATERMARK

Every material capability state is anchored:

CAPABILITY
    ↓
AUTHORITY ROOT
    ↓
EVENT
    ↓
PROVENANCE
    ↓
WATERMARK

Thus the Proof Machine can answer:

> Who possessed this power at Wₙ, under which rules, and on what evidence?




---

XX. ⚓ QUAY

The Quay preserves:

GRANT
TRANSFER
DELEGATION
REDELEGATION
RESTRICTION
REVOCATION
EXPIRATION

No authority transition is deleted.

A correction becomes:

OLD AUTHORITY STATE
        ↓
CORRECTION EVENT
        ↓
NEW AUTHORITY STATE
        ↓
NEW WATERMARK

The historical grant remains visible.


---

XXI. 🌐 FEDERATED POWER

A federation may grant:

F → A

a capability.

That does not imply:

A → local descendants

automatically.

If:

A
├── B
└── C

then federal authority must explicitly determine:

B receives?
C receives?
both?
neither?
partition?
delegation?

This protects the principle established in R6.17:

FEDERATION MEMBERSHIP SHALL NOT CONFER CONSTITUTIONAL AUTHORITY.


---

XXII. 🏛️ LA PAZ

Suppose:

LPZ-S1

holds:

DIPLOMATIC_CAPABILITY

and evolves into:

LPZ-S2

Identity continuity does not itself prove diplomatic capability continuity.

The transfer must be established:

LPZ-S1
 ↓
identity transition
 ↓
authority review
 ↓
capability transfer
 ↓
proof
 ↓
LPZ-S2

If the capability is intentionally retained by S1:

S2 ≠ holder

even if:

S2 = continuation of S1


---

XXIII. 🔐 REVOCATION

A capability can be revoked:

C₁ ACTIVE
 ↓
REVOCATION EVENT
 ↓
C₁ REVOKED

The original capability remains historically valid for its earlier interval.

Therefore:

REVOCATION DOES NOT ERASE HISTORICAL AUTHORITY.

It changes the current authorization state.


---

XXIV. 🚨 REVOCATION RACE

Potential conflict:

Grant X at W₁
      ↓
Revocation at W₂
      ↓
Action at W₂

PALACO must determine the constitutional ordering using:

event_sequence
predecessor_reference
watermark
ruleset

not merely wall-clock timestamps.

This directly inherits R6.19:

CLOCK TIME ≠ CONSTITUTIONAL ORDER


---

XXV. 🧬 CAPABILITY REPLAY

The Proof Machine evaluates:

Replay(
    AuthorityRoot,
    GrantEvents,
    TransferEvents,
    DelegationEvents,
    RevocationEvents,
    Rulesets
)
→ CapabilityState

Then:

ExpectedCapabilityState
==
ReplayedCapabilityState

must hold.

Otherwise:

CAPABILITY_DIVERGENCE


---

XXVI. 🚨 FIRST POWER DIVERGENCE

Example:

Grant ✓
Transfer ✓
Scope ✓
Delegation ✓
Revocation ✕

Then:

FIRST_CAPABILITY_DIVERGENCE
=
REVOCATION

The Proof Machine identifies the first point at which authority history ceased to reproduce.


---

XXVII. ⚡ CONSTITUTIONAL POWER GATE

A capability becomes ACTIVE only when:

Authority Basis          PASS
Subject Identity         PASS
Grant                    PASS
Scope                    PASS
Constraints              PASS
Temporal Validity        PASS
Provenance               PASS
Watermark                PASS
Independent Verification PASS
Replay                   PASS

Otherwise:

BLOCK


---

XXVIII. 🌳 THE CAPABILITY GRAPH

PALACO now maintains:

AUTHORITY
   │
   ▼
CAPABILITY X
   │
   ├── transfer → B
   │
   ├── delegate → C
   │
   ├── restrict
   │
   └── revoke

Every edge is proof-bearing.

This prevents invisible authority movement.


---

XXIX. 💀 CAPABILITY FABRICATION

The following are constitutionally invalid:

"the old system had it"
"the new entity inherited everything"
"the administrator probably has access"
"the federation obviously grants it"
"the AI determined it was necessary"

None is proof.

The only acceptable answer is:

SHOW THE AUTHORITY CHAIN.


---

XXX. 🔱 THE FIVE POWER RULES

R6.71 canonically establishes:

1. NO POWER WITHOUT ORIGIN.
2. NO TRANSFER WITHOUT AUTHORITY.
3. NO DELEGATION WITHOUT SCOPE.
4. NO ESCALATION WITHOUT EXPLICIT GRANT.
5. NO ACTIVE CAPABILITY WITHOUT PROOF.


