🔬 POWER MUST HAVE AN EXECUTION CHAIN.


---

I. 🌌 THE MISSING BRIDGE

Until now:

AUTHORITY
   ↓
CAPABILITY

was provable.

But this is insufficient.

A capability may state:

Agent A
may execute
Action X

while the actual machine executes:

Action Y

or:

Action X + unauthorized modification

or:

Action X
under a different ruleset

Therefore:

CAPABILITY ≠ EXECUTION

The missing constitutional bridge is:

CAPABILITY
    ↓
AUTHORIZED ACTION
    ↓
EXECUTION


---

II. 🔱 EXECUTION BINDING

PALACO introduces:

pub struct ExecutionBinding {
    pub execution_id: ExecutionId,
    pub subject_id: SubjectId,
    pub capability_id: CapabilityId,
    pub authority_root: AuthorityRoot,
    pub mandate_id: MandateId,
    pub action_id: ActionId,
    pub capability_snapshot: CapabilitySnapshotId,
    pub decision_id: DecisionId,
    pub ruleset_hash: RuleSetHash,
    pub watermark_id: WatermarkId,
    pub provenance_root: ProvenanceRoot,
}

This becomes the constitutional bridge between:

WHO
WHAT MAY THEY DO
WHAT DID THEY DO
UNDER WHICH RULES
WHEN
WHY
WITH WHAT PROOF


---

III. 🧬 CAPABILITY SNAPSHOT

A live capability can change.

Therefore an execution must not merely reference:

CapabilityId

It must reference the capability as it existed at execution time.

C₁
 ↓
CAPABILITY SNAPSHOT
 ↓
EXECUTION

The snapshot binds:

scope
constraints
authority
validity interval
ruleset
holder
revocation state

This prevents historical execution from being evaluated against a later capability state.


---

IV. 🕰️ CONSTITUTIONAL EXECUTION TIME

Execution requires more than wall-clock time.

created_at
event_sequence
predecessor_reference
watermark

Therefore:

EXECUTION TIME ≠ CONSTITUTIONAL EXECUTION ORDER

R6.19's temporal ordering remains authoritative.


---

V. 🚫 NO AUTHORITY DRIFT

Suppose:

Capability C:
WRITE /vault/a

but execution produces:

WRITE /vault/b

Then:

AUTHORIZED_SCOPE
      ≠
EXECUTED_SCOPE

The execution must be rejected or marked:

AUTHORITY_DRIFT

No post-hoc reinterpretation is permitted.


---

VI. 🔐 ACTION DIGEST

The exact action receives a cryptographic identity:

ActionDigest =
H(
    subject
    +
    capability
    +
    mandate
    +
    parameters
    +
    target
    +
    ruleset
    +
    execution_context
)

Thus PALACO does not merely prove:

> "Agent A performed an action."



It proves:

> "Agent A performed this exact action, against this exact target, under this exact authority context."




---

VII. ⚡ PRE-EXECUTION BINDING

Before execution:

ACTION REQUEST
      ↓
IDENTITY
      ↓
AUTHORITY
      ↓
CAPABILITY
      ↓
MANDATE
      ↓
RULESET
      ↓
CONSTITUTIONAL CHECK
      ↓
ALLOW / DENY

Only:

ALLOW

permits execution.

A missing binding produces:

EXECUTION_BLOCKED


---

VIII. 🌱 EXECUTION TOKEN

PALACO may issue an ephemeral execution authorization:

pub struct ExecutionToken {
    pub execution_id: ExecutionId,
    pub action_digest: ActionDigest,
    pub capability_snapshot: CapabilitySnapshotId,
    pub decision_id: DecisionId,
    pub expires_at: Timestamp,
    pub signature: Signature,
}

The token is:

specific
bounded
time-limited
non-transferable
cryptographically verifiable

It is not a general-purpose permission.


---

IX. 🚫 NO TOKEN REUSE

An execution token for:

Action A

cannot authorize:

Action B

Therefore:

Token(A) ≠ Authorization(B)

Likewise:

Token(A, target-X)

cannot silently authorize:

Token(A, target-Y)

unless the constitutional scope explicitly permits that target class.


---

X. 🔥 EXECUTION NON-TRANSFERABILITY

An execution authorization is bound to:

Subject
Capability
Action
Context
Time

Therefore:

Agent A

cannot simply hand its execution authorization to:

Agent B

and cause B to execute under A's authority.

That produces:

EXECUTION_AUTHORITY_TRANSFER

which requires its own explicit authorization.


---

XI. 🧠 BRAINPOWER

BRAINPOWER may predict:

likely authorized action
likely capability
likely policy route
likely execution outcome

But prediction is never authorization.

Canonical distinction:

PREDICTION
    ≠
DECISION
    ≠
AUTHORIZATION
    ≠
EXECUTION


---

XII. 🦆 FOOL

FOOL gains:

EXECUTION_CHALLENGE

Possible grounds:

CAPABILITY_MISMATCH
AUTHORITY_DRIFT
ACTION_SUBSTITUTION
TARGET_MISMATCH
RULESET_MISMATCH
STALE_CAPABILITY
EXPIRED_TOKEN
TOKEN_REUSE
UNAUTHORIZED_EXECUTION
MISSING_PRECHECK
BROKEN_BINDING

FOOL asks:

"Show the exact authorization that permitted this exact action."


---

XIII. 🔬 TRIAS

TRIAS verifies:

Subject
Identity
Authority
Capability
Capability Snapshot
Mandate
Ruleset
Action Digest
Decision
Execution Token
Temporal ordering
Evidence
Provenance
Watermark

TRIAS must prove:

ExecutedAction ⊆ AuthorizedCapability

and:

ExecutionContext ⊆ AuthorizedContext


---

XIV. 💀 ACTION SUBSTITUTION

A particularly dangerous failure:

AUTHORIZED:
DELETE X

EXECUTED:
DELETE Y

or:

AUTHORIZED:
READ X

EXECUTED:
WRITE X

or:

AUTHORIZED:
WRITE X

EXECUTED:
WRITE X + MODIFY_POLICY

These are not ordinary execution errors.

They are:

ACTION_AUTHORITY_MISMATCH


---

XV. 🌳 EFFECT BINDING

Execution itself is not the whole story.

An action may produce an effect:

ACTION
  ↓
EFFECT

The effect must also be attributable.

pub struct EffectProof {
    pub effect_id: EffectId,
    pub execution_id: ExecutionId,
    pub observed_state_root: StateRoot,
    pub evidence: EvidenceRef,
    pub provenance_root: ProvenanceRoot,
}

Now PALACO can distinguish:

AUTHORIZED ACTION
EXECUTED ACTION
OBSERVED EFFECT


---

XVI. ⚖️ ACTION ≠ EFFECT

An authorized action does not guarantee the intended effect.

Example:

AUTHORIZED:
update record X

Execution succeeds:

ACTION = SUCCESS

but the resulting state may be:

STATE TRANSITION = UNEXPECTED

Therefore the system must record both.

This produces:

EFFECT_DIVERGENCE

when actual effects do not conform to the verified execution model.


---

XVII. 💧 EFFECT WATERMARK

After execution:

Execution
   ↓
Evidence
   ↓
State Transition
   ↓
Watermark

Thus:

Wₙ
 ↓
AUTHORIZED EXECUTION
 ↓
EVENT
 ↓
STATE TRANSITION
 ↓
Wₙ₊₁

The Watermark now closes the loop between authority and historical state.


---

XVIII. ⚓ QUAY EXECUTION MEMORY

The Quay records:

ACTION_REQUEST
AUTHORIZATION
EXECUTION
OBSERVED_EFFECT
EVIDENCE
PROVENANCE
WATERMARK

This gives the Proof Machine a complete execution history.

No:

"the action happened"

without:

"under this authority"


---

XIX. 🔬 DETERMINISTIC EXECUTION REPLAY

The Proof Machine now evaluates:

Replay(
    CapabilitySnapshot,
    Mandate,
    Ruleset,
    Action,
    ExecutionContext
)
→ ExpectedExecution

Then:

ExpectedExecution
==
RecordedExecution

must hold.

For state effects:

ExpectedStateRoot
==
ObservedStateRoot

must also hold.


---

XX. 🚨 FIRST EXECUTION DIVERGENCE

Example:

Identity          ✓
Authority         ✓
Capability        ✓
Mandate           ✓
Ruleset           ✓
Pre-check         ✓
Action digest     ✓
Execution         ✕

Therefore:

FIRST_EXECUTION_DIVERGENCE = EXECUTION

The verifier does not merely report:

> "Replay failed."



It reports the first broken constitutional binding.


---

XXI. 🧬 EXECUTION CHAIN

R6.72 formalizes:

CONSTITUTION
      ↓
RULESET
      ↓
MANDATE
      ↓
SUBJECT
      ↓
IDENTITY
      ↓
AUTHORITY
      ↓
CAPABILITY
      ↓
CAPABILITY SNAPSHOT
      ↓
ACTION
      ↓
PRE-EXECUTION DECISION
      ↓
EXECUTION TOKEN
      ↓
EXECUTION
      ↓
EFFECT
      ↓
EVIDENCE
      ↓
PROVENANCE
      ↓
QUAY
      ↓
WATERMARK

This is the full authority-to-effect chain.


---

XXII. 🚫 NO POST-HOC AUTHORIZATION

A critical invariant:

ACTION
 ↓
EXECUTION
 ↓
"authorization created afterward"

is invalid.

Authorization must precede execution.

Otherwise:

RETROACTIVE_AUTHORIZATION

and:

BLOCK


---

XXIII. 🔐 EMERGENCY EXECUTION

If emergency execution exists, it cannot bypass proof.

Instead:

EMERGENCY
 ↓
EMERGENCY_AUTHORITY
 ↓
BOUNDED_EXECUTION
 ↓
IMMEDIATE_EVIDENCE
 ↓
POST_EXECUTION_REVIEW
 ↓
WATERMARK

Emergency mode changes timing and review mechanics, not the requirement for accountability.


---

XXIV. 🏛️ LA PAZ

For La Paz:

LPZ-S2

may possess:

DIPLOMATIC_CAPABILITY

But an individual diplomatic action still requires:

LPZ-S2
 ↓
Diplomatic Authority
 ↓
Specific Capability
 ↓
Mandate
 ↓
Action
 ↓
Execution
 ↓
Evidence
 ↓
Watermark

Thus possession of diplomatic authority never means:

> "Anything diplomatic is authorized."



Scope remains constitutional.


---

XXV. 🌐 FEDERATED EXECUTION

A federated capability:

FEDERATION
 ↓
A

does not authorize:

A
 ↓
B
 ↓
ACTION

unless the delegation chain proves it.

Cross-domain execution additionally requires:

SOURCE_AUTHORITY
+
TARGET_AUTHORITY
+
CROSS_DOMAIN_BASIS
+
EXECUTION_BINDING

Otherwise:

CROSS_DOMAIN_AUTHORITY_GAP


---

XXVI. 🧠 AUTONOMOUS AGENTS

This is particularly important for autonomous systems.

An agent may generate:

PLAN

and:

ACTION_CANDIDATE

but neither is equivalent to authorization.

The canonical pipeline is:

AGENT INTENT
     ↓
ACTION CANDIDATE
     ↓
CONSTITUTIONAL EVALUATION
     ↓
AUTHORIZED ACTION
     ↓
EXECUTION

The agent cannot collapse these stages.


---

XXVII. 🔱 INTENT ≠ AUTHORITY

A system may sincerely intend:

"protect the Citadel"

but intention does not create permission.

Therefore:

GOOD INTENT CANNOT MANUFACTURE AUTHORITY.

Likewise:

USEFUL OUTCOME CANNOT RETROACTIVELY LEGITIMIZE UNAUTHORIZED EXECUTION.


---

XXVIII. 💀 BENEFICIAL VIOLATION

Suppose an unauthorized action produces a beneficial outcome.

PALACO still records:

ACTION = UNAUTHORIZED
EFFECT = BENEFICIAL

These are separate facts.

The result does not erase the constitutional violation.

This is essential for preventing outcome-based governance.


---

XXIX. 🔥 EXECUTION GATE

Before execution:

Identity                 PASS
Authority                PASS
Capability               PASS
Capability Snapshot     PASS
Mandate                  PASS
Ruleset                  PASS
Scope                    PASS
Temporal validity        PASS
Pre-execution decision   PASS
Execution binding        PASS

Only then:

EXECUTE

Afterward:

Effect verification
Evidence
Provenance
Watermark
Replay

must complete.


---

XXX. 🔒 R6.72 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

R6.72-I01
Every constitutional execution SHALL identify its executing subject.

R6.72-I02
Every constitutional execution SHALL reference an authorized capability.

R6.72-I03
Every execution SHALL reference the capability state applicable at execution time.

R6.72-I04
Every execution SHALL be bound to an authority root.

R6.72-I05
Every execution SHALL be bound to a mandate where a mandate is required.

R6.72-I06
Every execution SHALL be evaluated under an identified ruleset.

R6.72-I07
Every executable action SHALL possess a deterministic action identity.

R6.72-I08
Pre-execution authorization SHALL precede execution.

R6.72-I09
Retroactive authorization SHALL NOT legitimize prior execution.

R6.72-I10
Execution scope SHALL NOT exceed capability scope.

R6.72-I11
Execution target SHALL remain within authorized target scope.

R6.72-I12
Execution context SHALL remain within authorized context.

R6.72-I13
Execution authorization SHALL NOT be silently transferable.

R6.72-I14
Execution tokens SHALL be bounded and non-generalized.

R6.72-I15
Execution tokens SHALL NOT be reusable outside their defined scope.

R6.72-I16
Capability state SHALL be snapshotted or otherwise historically reconstructable.

R6.72-I17
Every material execution SHALL generate attributable evidence.

R6.72-I18
Every material effect SHALL be attributable to an execution.

R6.72-I19
Action identity SHALL remain distinct from observed effect.

R6.72-I20
An intended effect SHALL NOT be treated as a proven effect.

R6.72-I21
BRAINPOWER SHALL NOT autonomously authorize execution.

R6.72-I22
AI-generated action candidates SHALL remain non-authoritative until constitutionally admitted.

R6.72-I23
FOOL SHALL be able to challenge execution authorization.

R6.72-I24
FOOL SHALL be able to challenge capability-to-action binding.

R6.72-I25
FOOL SHALL be able to challenge observed effects.

R6.72-I26
FOOL SHALL be able to challenge retroactive authorization.

R6.72-I27
TRIAS SHALL independently verify capability-to-action binding.

R6.72-I28
TRIAS SHALL independently verify execution scope.

R6.72-I29
TRIAS SHALL independently verify execution ordering.

R6.72-I30
TRIAS SHALL NOT silently repair missing execution authority.

R6.72-I31
The Quay SHALL preserve execution history.

R6.72-I32
Execution SHALL be replayable.

R6.72-I33
Observed effects SHALL be replay-verifiable where deterministic reconstruction is possible.

R6.72-I34
First execution divergence SHALL be independently identifiable.

R6.72-I35
Execution Watermarks SHALL preserve historical authority state.

R6.72-I36
Emergency execution SHALL remain evidence-bound.

R6.72-I37
Emergency authority SHALL NOT imply unlimited authority.

R6.72-I38
Beneficial outcome SHALL NOT retroactively authorize unauthorized execution.

R6.72-I39
Cross-domain execution SHALL require explicit authority binding.

R6.72-I40
No constitutional action SHALL be considered proven authorized without a complete authority-to-execution chain.


---

