XXX. 💥 THE BIG PRINCIPLE

R6.73 makes canonical:

> A system SHALL NOT attribute a constitutional consequence to an action merely because the consequence followed the action. The causal relationship SHALL be explicitly evidenced, provenance-bound, appropriately qualified, and independently verifiable according to the applicable proof standard.



Or, in the compressed PALACO form:

NO CAUSAL LINK → NO CAUSAL CLAIM.

And the deeper rule:

**OBSERVATION TELLS US WHAT CHANGED.

PROVENANCE TELLS US WHERE IT CAME FROM.
CAUSALITY TELLS US WHY WE MAY ATTRIBUTE THE CHANGE.**


---

XXXI. 🔒 R6.73 SEALED

The Watermark now does more than preserve time.

It anchors a chain:

PAST
 ↓
CONTINUITY
 ↓
IDENTITY
 ↓
AUTHORITY
 ↓
POWER
 ↓
ACTION
 ↓
EFFECT
 ↓
CAUSALITY
 ↓
CONSEQUENCE
 ↓
ACCOUNTABILITY

And this produces an important constitutional boundary:

💧 THE MACHINE MAY REMEMBER WHAT HAPPENED.

🔬 THE MACHINE MAY PROVE WHAT WAS AUTHORIZED.

⚡ THE MACHINE MAY PROVE WHAT WAS EXECUTED.

💥 THE MACHINE MAY ATTRIBUTE CONSEQUENCES ONLY WHERE CAUSALITY IS PROVEN.


---

☄️ R6.74 — THE NEXT BOUNDARY

One final problem now emerges naturally.

If an action can cause consequences, then some consequences may themselves create new obligations.

Therefore the next question is:

> When a proven action produces a constitutionally relevant consequence, how does PALACO deterministically derive the resulting duties, prohibitions, remediation requirements, liabilities, and deadlines—and prove that these obligations themselves originated from the correct causal event?



That opens:

☄️ R6.74 — CONSTITUTIONAL CONSEQUENCE-TO-OBLIGATION DERIVATION PROTOCOL

EXECUTION
   ↓
EFFECT
   ↓
CAUSALITY
   ↓
CONSEQUENCE
   ↓
OBLIGATION DERIVATION
   ↓
DUTY
   ↓
DEADLINE
   ↓
RESPONSIBLE SUBJECT
   ↓
EVIDENCE
   ↓
WATERMARK

The governing principle:

🌱 A PROVEN CONSEQUENCE MAY CREATE AN OBLIGATION — BUT THE OBLIGATION ITSELF MUST HAVE A PROVABLE CONSTITUTIONAL BASIS.

☄️🌱🦆💧⚓🔬🧠🔱🏛️❤️

**CAUSE CREATES CONSEQUENCE.

CONSEQUENCE MAY CREATE DUTY.
PROOF CONNECTS THEM ALL.**

☄️ R6.74 — CONSTITUTIONAL CONSEQUENCE-TO-OBLIGATION DERIVATION PROTOCOL

A CONSEQUENCE MAY CREATE A DUTY — BUT NEVER WITHOUT A PROVABLE CONSTITUTIONAL BASIS

Status: 🔒 CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD
Parent: R6.73 — Constitutional Effect, Consequence & Causality Proof Protocol
Core primitive: OBLIGATION_DERIVATION
Supporting primitives: CONSEQUENCE_RECORD, OBLIGATION_RECORD, DERIVATION_PROOF, DUTY_WATERMARK

Primary invariant

> No constitutional obligation SHALL become active unless its origin, legal/constitutional basis, triggering consequence, responsible subject, scope, and applicable temporal conditions are independently reconstructible.



R6.73 established:

💥 CAUSE MUST BE PROVEN.

R6.74 establishes:

⚖️ DUTY MUST BE DERIVED — NEVER INVENTED.


---

I. 🌌 THE NEXT LOGICAL STEP

The constitutional chain now reaches:

ACTION
   ↓
EXECUTION
   ↓
EFFECT
   ↓
CAUSALITY
   ↓
CONSEQUENCE

But a constitutional system does not merely observe consequences.

A consequence can trigger:

DUTY
REMEDIATION
REVIEW
DISCLOSURE
CONTAINMENT
CORRECTION
DEADLINE
ESCALATION

Therefore:

CONSEQUENCE
      ↓
?
      ↓
OBLIGATION

The ? must itself be proven.

That bridge becomes:

OBLIGATION_DERIVATION


---

II. 🔱 CONSEQUENCE ≠ OBLIGATION

A consequence does not automatically create a duty.

Example:

EVENT
 ↓
STATE CHANGE

does not by itself prove:

STATE CHANGE
 ↓
AGENT MUST ACT

The obligation requires a constitutional source:

CONSEQUENCE
      +
CONSTITUTIONAL BASIS
      +
APPLICABLE RULE
      +
TRIGGER CONDITION
      ↓
OBLIGATION


---

III. ⚖️ OBLIGATION OBJECT

PALACO introduces:

pub struct Obligation {
    pub obligation_id: ObligationId,
    pub subject_id: SubjectId,
    pub source_consequence: ConsequenceId,
    pub constitutional_basis: ConstitutionalBasisId,
    pub ruleset_hash: RuleSetHash,
    pub mandate_id: Option<MandateId>,
    pub obligation_type: ObligationType,
    pub scope: ObligationScope,
    pub created_at: Timestamp,
    pub due_at: Option<Timestamp>,
    pub provenance_root: ProvenanceRoot,
    pub watermark_id: WatermarkId,
    pub status: ObligationStatus,
}

The obligation therefore answers:

WHO must act?
WHY must they act?
WHAT must they do?
UNDER WHICH RULE?
BECAUSE OF WHICH CONSEQUENCE?
BY WHEN?
WITH WHAT PROOF?


---

IV. 🌳 DERIVATION FUNCTION

Conceptually:

Oₙ =
Derive(
    Cₙ,
    Constitution,
    RuleSet,
    Subject,
    Context
)

But only where the trigger conditions hold.

Thus:

Trigger(Cₙ, Rₙ) = TRUE

must be demonstrable.

Otherwise:

NO_OBLIGATION_DERIVED


---

V. 🔬 DERIVATION PROOF

Every material obligation receives a derivation proof:

pub struct DerivationProof {
    pub derivation_id: DerivationId,
    pub consequence_id: ConsequenceId,
    pub rule_id: RuleId,
    pub constitutional_basis: ConstitutionalBasisId,
    pub subject_id: SubjectId,
    pub trigger_digest: TriggerDigest,
    pub derived_obligation: ObligationId,
    pub evidence: EvidenceRef,
    pub verifier: VerifierId,
}

This creates:

CONSEQUENCE
    ↓
TRIGGER
    ↓
RULE
    ↓
DERIVATION
    ↓
OBLIGATION


---

VI. 🚫 NO DUTY BY INTUITION

PALACO SHALL NOT produce:

> “Someone should probably fix this.”



That may be a useful recommendation.

It is not yet a constitutional obligation.

The distinction becomes:

RECOMMENDATION
   ≠
PROPOSAL
   ≠
DERIVED OBLIGATION

Only the last one carries binding constitutional status.


