                   ↓
               OBLIGATION
                   ↓
              FULFILLMENT
                   ↓
                EVIDENCE
                   ↓
               PROVENANCE
                   ↓
                  QUAY
                   ↓
               WATERMARK
                   ↓
                 REPLAY
                   ↓
             SECOND WITNESS
                   ↓
               CHALLENGE
                   ↓
              ADJUDICATION
                   ↓
               CORRECTION
                   ↓
              NEW WATERMARK
                   ↓
               CONTINUITY

And then the next lawful action may begin the cycle again.


---

XXXI. 💥 THE BIG PRINCIPLE

R6.74 makes canonical:

> A constitutionally relevant consequence SHALL NOT become a binding obligation merely because an agent, verifier, or AI considers such an obligation desirable. The obligation SHALL be derived from an identifiable constitutional basis through a reproducible trigger and derivation chain.



Compressed:

NO BASIS → NO DUTY.

And even stronger:

NO DERIVATION → NO OBLIGATION.


---

XXXII. 🔒 R6.74 SEALED

The Proof Machine can now follow the entire normative lifecycle:

POWER
 ↓
ACTION
 ↓
EFFECT
 ↓
CAUSE
 ↓
CONSEQUENCE
 ↓
DUTY
 ↓
FULFILLMENT

The constitutional distinction is absolute:

WHAT MAY HAPPEN
        ≠
WHAT HAPPENED
        ≠
WHAT IT CAUSED
        ≠
WHAT MUST NOW BE DONE

Each transition requires its own proof boundary.

⚖️ PALACO DOES NOT INVENT DUTIES FROM OUTCOMES.

It derives them from constitutional law.


---

☄️ R6.75 — NEXT FRONTIER

One problem now becomes unavoidable.

Once obligations exist, multiple obligations can collide:

O₁ → ACT
O₂ → DO NOT ACT
O₃ → ACT BEFORE T
O₄ → PRESERVE STATE
O₅ → MODIFY STATE

What happens when two independently valid constitutional obligations become mutually incompatible?

The system cannot simply choose one.

That opens:

☄️ R6.75 — CONSTITUTIONAL OBLIGATION CONFLICT, PRIORITY & RECONCILIATION PROTOCOL

OBLIGATION A
      │
      ├──────┐
      │      │
      ▼      ▼
   ACTION A  ACTION B
      │      │
      └──┬───┘
         ▼
   CONFLICT DETECTED
         ↓
   CONSTITUTIONAL ANALYSIS
         ↓
   PRIORITY / COMPATIBILITY
         ↓
   RECONCILIATION
         ↓
   PROOF
         ↓
   WATERMARK

De volgende grens wordt daarmee:

🔥 **VALID DUTIES MAY CONFLICT.

CONFLICT MUST BE PROVEN.
PRIORITY MUST BE JUSTIFIED.
RECONCILIATION MUST BE AUDITABLE.**

☄️🌱🦆💧⚓🔬🧠⚖️🔱🏛️❤️

**THE CONSTITUTION DOES NOT ONLY GOVERN ACTION.

IT ALSO GOVERNS WHAT HAPPENS WHEN VALID DUTIES COLLIDE.**

☄️ R6.75 — CONSTITUTIONAL OBLIGATION CONFLICT, PRIORITY & RECONCILIATION PROTOCOL

VALID DUTIES MAY COLLIDE — BUT NO DUTY MAY SILENTLY OVERRIDE ANOTHER

Status: 🔒 CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD
Parent: R6.74 — Constitutional Consequence-to-Obligation Derivation Protocol
Core primitive: OBLIGATION_CONFLICT
Supporting primitives: PRIORITY_PROOF, RECONCILIATION_PROOF, CONFLICT_WATERMARK

Primary invariant

> When two or more valid constitutional obligations cannot be simultaneously satisfied within the same admissible execution context, the conflict SHALL be explicitly detected, classified, adjudicated, and provenance-bound before any obligation is suppressed, deferred, prioritized, or reconciled.



R6.74 established:

⚖️ NO DERIVATION → NO OBLIGATION.

R6.75 establishes:

🔥 NO SILENT OVERRIDE → NO LEGITIMATE PRIORITY.


---

I. 🌌 THE NEW PROBLEM

PALACO can now prove:

O₁ = VALID
O₂ = VALID

Yet:

O₁ → ACTION A
O₂ → ACTION B

may be mutually incompatible.

Example:

O₁:
Preserve immutable historical state.

O₂:
Correct the affected state.

Both may be legitimate.

The constitutional question is therefore not:

> “Which obligation do we prefer?”



but:

> “Can these obligations actually coexist, and if not, what constitutional rule governs their reconciliation?”




---

II. 🔱 CONFLICT ≠ DISAGREEMENT

PALACO distinguishes:

DISAGREEMENT
    ≠
COMPETITION
    ≠
INCOMPATIBILITY
    ≠
CONSTITUTIONAL CONFLICT

A conflict exists only when simultaneous fulfillment is impossible or materially incompatible within the defined context.

Conceptually:

Compatible(O₁, O₂) = TRUE

means no conflict.

Compatible(O₁, O₂) = FALSE

creates:

OBLIGATION_CONFLICT


---

III. 🧬 OBLIGATION CONFLICT OBJECT

pub struct ObligationConflict {
    pub conflict_id: ConflictId,
    pub obligations: Vec<ObligationId>,
    pub conflict_type: ConflictType,
    pub context: ConflictContext,
    pub detected_at: Timestamp,
    pub detection_evidence: EvidenceRef,
    pub provenance_root: ProvenanceRoot,
    pub watermark_id: WatermarkId,
    pub status: ConflictStatus,
}

The conflict becomes a first-class constitutional object.

It cannot remain an undocumented runtime condition.


---

