XV. 🌱 RECONCILIATION STRATEGIES

SimultaneousFulfillment
SequentialFulfillment
ScopedFulfillment
TemporalDeferral
PartialFulfillment
ConditionalFulfillment
PriorityResolution
DelegatedFulfillment
ConstitutionalEscalation
IrreconcilableConflict

The strategy itself must be justified.


---

XVI. 🕰️ TEMPORAL RECONCILIATION

Two obligations may conflict only because they require the same resource simultaneously.

Example:

O₁ → act before T₂
O₂ → preserve system until T₃

If:

T₁ < T₂ < T₃

a valid sequence may satisfy both.

Thus:

CONFLICT
 ↓
TEMPORAL ANALYSIS
 ↓
SEQUENCING
 ↓
NO SUPPRESSION


---

XVII. 💀 IRRECONCILABLE CONFLICT

Sometimes no lawful solution exists.

Then:

O₁
O₂
 ↓
NO_VALID_RECONCILIATION

produces:

IRRECONCILABLE_CONFLICT

The system must stop, escalate, or invoke the constitutionally defined exceptional process.

It must not choose silently.


---

XVIII. 🚫 DEADLOCK ≠ PERMISSION

A system stuck between obligations cannot infer:

> “Therefore I may do whatever is operationally convenient.”



Instead:

CONFLICT
 ↓
NO_VALID_RESOLUTION
 ↓
BLOCK / ESCALATE

This is a major constitutional safety boundary.


---

XIX. 🔥 LEAST-CONSTITUTIONAL-DAMAGE PRINCIPLE

Where the constitution explicitly permits reconciliation optimization, PALACO may select the solution that minimizes irreversible constitutional damage.

Conceptually:

R* = argmin(
    irreversible_harm
    +
    authority_violation
    +
    provenance_loss
    +
    constitutional_damage
)

But the optimization criterion itself must be constitutionally authorized.

It cannot become an independent source of law.


---

XX. 💧 CONFLICT WATERMARK

A conflict resolution becomes historical state:

Wₙ
 ↓
CONFLICT DETECTED
 ↓
ANALYSIS
 ↓
RECONCILIATION / PRIORITY
 ↓
DECISION
 ↓
EXECUTION
 ↓
Wₙ₊₁

Thus future verifiers can reconstruct:

what conflicted
why it conflicted
what rule applied
what was chosen
why it was chosen
what consequences followed


---

XXI. ⚓ QUAY

The Quay stores:

OBLIGATIONS
CONFLICT
ANALYSIS
PRIORITY
RECONCILIATION
DECISION
EXECUTION
EVIDENCE
WATERMARK

Nothing disappears simply because the conflict was resolved.


---

XXII. 🧬 CONFLICT CORRECTION

If later evidence shows:

O₁ > O₂

was incorrectly established:

PRIORITY_DECISION
 ↓
CHALLENGE
 ↓
ADJUDICATION
 ↓
CORRECTION
 ↓
NEW RESOLUTION
 ↓
NEW WATERMARK

The historical priority decision remains preserved.


---

XXIII. 🌐 FEDERATED CONFLICT

Suppose:

DOMAIN A:
O₁

DOMAIN B:
O₂

and both are valid.

Federation cannot simply impose:

A > B

because:

FEDERATION DOES NOT CREATE SUPERIOR CONSTITUTIONAL AUTHORITY BY DEFAULT.

The federation must identify:

shared constitutional basis
jurisdictional boundary
applicable agreement
reconciliation mechanism

Otherwise:

FEDERATED_CONFLICT_UNRESOLVED


