XVI. 🔱 R6.97 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

R6.97-I01
A precedent SHALL reference an identifiable originating determination.

R6.97-I02
A precedent SHALL preserve its evidence lineage.

R6.97-I03
A precedent SHALL preserve its authority basis.

R6.97-I04
A precedent SHALL possess explicit scope.

R6.97-I05
A precedent SHALL possess temporal context.

R6.97-I06
Precedent SHALL NOT automatically constitute current truth.

R6.97-I07
Precedent SHALL NOT silently expand constitutional authority.

R6.97-I08
Repeated practice SHALL NOT automatically create constitutional legitimacy.

R6.97-I09
BRAINPOWER MAY identify precedent relevance but SHALL NOT independently establish precedent authority.

R6.97-I10
FOOL SHALL be able to challenge precedent applicability.

R6.97-I11
TRIAS SHALL verify precedent authority and scope.

R6.97-I12
Quay SHALL preserve precedent history.

R6.97-I13
Precedent references SHALL be watermarkable.

R6.97-I14
Distinguishing a precedent SHALL NOT erase the precedent.

R6.97-I15
Overruling a precedent SHALL NOT erase its historical existence.

R6.97-I16
Superseded precedent SHALL remain replayable where material.

R6.97-I17
Current evidence SHALL be evaluated independently where materially different.

R6.97-I18
Temporal changes SHALL be explicitly considered.

R6.97-I19
Constitutional consistency SHALL NOT be achieved by suppressing contradictory history.

R6.97-I20
No precedent SHALL create authority broader than its originating authority.

R6.97-I21
No precedent SHALL silently modify unrelated obligations.

R6.97-I22
Current determinations SHALL remain attributable to their actual evidence and authority.


