En de verifier kan meteen zien:

naam → type → bewijs → bevoegdheid → identiteit → lineage.


---

XVIII. 🧠 THE SEMANTIC SAFETY RULE

R6.104 voegt een belangrijke veiligheidslaag toe:

> No natural-language phrase SHALL silently acquire a stronger constitutional meaning than its determined semantic scope permits.



Dus:

"forgiven"

mag niet automatisch worden geïnterpreteerd als:

"sanction terminated"

en:

"resolved"

mag niet automatisch betekenen:

"breach erased"

en:

"trusted"

mag niet automatisch betekenen:

"authorized"

Dit is de semantic firewall.


---

XIX. 🕊️ FORGIVENESS × NOMENCLATURE

R6.84/R6.85 worden hiermee nog sterker.

Stel:

BREACH B₁

wordt:

PARDONED

Later wordt het display label:

"Resolved Atlantis Incident"

Dat mag nooit de verifier laten concluderen:

NO BREACH

De onderliggende identity blijft:

B₁

De historical record:

VERIFIED BREACH

De consequence:

PARDONED

De display name:

Resolved Atlantis Incident

Alle vier kunnen tegelijk bestaan.


---

XX. 🌱 R6.104 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

R6.104-I01
Names SHALL remain distinguishable from object identity.

R6.104-I02
Names SHALL remain distinguishable from classification.

R6.104-I03
Names SHALL remain distinguishable from authority.

R6.104-I04
A rename SHALL NOT silently create a new historical object.

R6.104-I05
A rename SHALL NOT erase prior names from provenance.

R6.104-I06
Object identity SHALL remain stable across ordinary nomenclature changes.

R6.104-I07
Watermarks SHALL remain independent of mutable display names.

R6.104-I08
Linguistic synonymy SHALL NOT silently establish constitutional equivalence.

R6.104-I09
BRAINPOWER MAY detect and propose semantic equivalence but SHALL NOT autonomously determine constitutional effect.

R6.104-I10
Material semantic ambiguity SHALL remain explicit until determined.

R6.104-I11
FOOL SHALL be able to inspect naming history.

R6.104-I12
TRIAS SHALL verify material naming transitions where authority is required.

R6.104-I13
Quay SHALL preserve material nomenclature history.

R6.104-I14
Taxonomy version changes SHALL NOT silently mutate object identity.

R6.104-I15
Reclassification SHALL remain distinguishable from renaming.

R6.104-I16
Renaming SHALL remain distinguishable from constitutional state transition.

R6.104-I17
Deprecated names SHALL remain historically discoverable where required.

R6.104-I18
Name collisions SHALL NOT be silently resolved through assumption.

R6.104-I19
A natural-language phrase SHALL NOT acquire constitutional authority merely through repetition.

R6.104-I20
No nomenclature mechanism SHALL delete verified constitutional provenance.


---

XXI. ☄️ THE FOUR GREAT DISTINCTIONS

R6.101 → R6.104 heeft nu vier fundamentele onderscheidingen opgebouwd:

CLASSIFICATION
"What kind of thing?"

IDENTITY
"Which exact thing?"

PROVENANCE
"Where did it come from?"

AUTHORITY
"Who may act upon it?"

En daaronder:

Z.A.N.D.
"What can we actually establish?"


