I. 🌳 THE NEXT DISCOVERY

R6.118 established that constitutional entities can grow.

R6.119 asks the next inevitable question:

> What happens when things growing inside the same constitutional garden begin to affect one another?



A living system is not merely:

SPECIMEN
   ↓
GROWTH

It is:

SPECIMEN
   ↕
RELATION
   ↕
SPECIMEN

Therefore:

RELATIONSHIP BECOMES A FIRST-CLASS CONSTITUTIONAL OBJECT.


---

II. 🌿 RELATION IS NOT AUTHORITY

The foundational distinction:

RELATION
   ≠
AUTHORITY

Two actors may:

cooperate;

depend on each other;

exchange information;

share resources;

compete;

disagree;

coexist;


without either actor acquiring constitutional authority over the other.

Thus:

A ↔ B

does not imply:

A > B


---

III. 🧬 THE RELATIONAL SPECIMEN

A relationship itself becomes provenance-bearing:

RELATIONSHIP
├── RelationId
├── Subject
├── Counterparty
├── Type
├── Evidence
├── AuthorityBasis
├── Scope
├── TemporalScope
├── Constraints
├── Watermark
└── Lineage

Conceptually:

pub struct ConstitutionalRelation {
    pub relation_id: RelationId,
    pub subject: ActorId,
    pub counterparty: ActorId,
    pub relation_type: RelationType,
    pub evidence_root: EvidenceRoot,
    pub authority_basis: Option<AuthorityReference>,
    pub scope: Scope,
    pub temporal_scope: TemporalScope,
    pub watermark: WatermarkId,
}


---

IV. 🌱 THE RELATION TAXONOMY

PALACO now distinguishes:

RELATION
├── COOPERATION
├── DEPENDENCY
├── EXCHANGE
├── SUPPORT
├── DELEGATION
├── REPRESENTATION
├── COMPETITION
├── CONFLICT
├── ALLIANCE
├── TRUST
└── GOVERNANCE

But these labels are descriptive categories.

They do not automatically grant permissions.


---

V. ⚖️ RELATIONAL SEMANTICS

A crucial rule:

> A relation describes how entities are connected. It does not by itself establish what either entity is permitted to do.



Therefore:

COOPERATION
≠
AUTHORIZATION

TRUST
≠
PRIVILEGE

ALLIANCE
≠
SOVEREIGNTY

DEPENDENCY
≠
SUBORDINATION


