VIII. 🚨 REVOCATION PROPAGATION

Now comes the difficult case.

Suppose:

A → B → C

Then A revokes B.

What happens to C?

The answer:

A
 ↓
REVOCATION
 ↓
B AUTHORITY ENDS
 ↓
C's derived authority
 ↓
NO LONGER VALID

But again:

REVOCATION
≠
ERASURE

The historical chain remains:

A → B → C → ACTION
          ↑
      historical

The verifier evaluates the action according to the state that existed at execution time.


---

IX. ⏳ TEMPORAL CASCADE

Consider:

A → B
10:00–18:00

B → C
12:00–16:00

Then:

C authority = 12:00–16:00

At:

16:01

C is no longer authorized.

At:

18:01

B is also no longer authorized.

The system never says:

> “B was once trusted, therefore C remains trusted.”



Instead:

AUTHORITY(t)

is calculated from the historical state.


---

X. 🌐 FEDERATED SUB-DELEGATION

Across Citadel boundaries:

CITADEL A
   │
   └── A → B
             │
             ▼
        CITADEL B
             │
